window.PHYS={"unit":{"unit":3,"title":"Heart, Vessels, Blood, Immunity and Breathing","chapters":"Sherwood Chapters 9 to 13","course":"BIO 141 Human Physiology","teacher":"Dr. Khan Quintana","app":"Physiology Practice","key":"physio.practice.u3.v1","order_blurb":"The cardiac cycle, the baroreflex, clotting, breathing and more","exam":{"name":"Exam 3","date":"2026-11-19","label":"Thu Nov 19"},"units":[{"label":"← Unit 2","title":"Go back to Unit 2: nerves, senses and muscle","href":"unit2.html"}]},"groups":[["9","Chapter 9 · The heart"],["10","Chapter 10 · Blood vessels and blood pressure"],["11","Chapter 11 · Blood"],["12","Chapter 12 · Body defenses"],["13","Chapter 13 · The respiratory system"]],"topics":[{"id":"kc1","ch":"9","title":"Cardiac output: CO = HR × SV","core":"Cardiac output (CO) is how much blood one ventricle pumps each minute: CO = HR × SV. Stroke volume (SV) is what leaves with each beat: SV = EDV − ESV, the volume when the ventricle is full (end-diastolic volume) minus the volume left behind after it squeezes (end-systolic volume). A typical resting ventricle fills to about 120 mL, keeps about 50 mL, and pushes out about 70 mL per beat, which adds up to about 5 L/min.","trap":"Multiplying heart rate by EDV. EDV is everything in the ventricle, not what left it, and the ventricle never empties completely. Always subtract ESV first to get SV, then multiply by HR.","q":"Write the two cardiac output equations. Why is HR × EDV the wrong answer?","a":"CO = HR × SV, and SV = EDV − ESV. EDV is the full volume, but ESV always stays behind, so only EDV − ESV is pumped out each beat."},{"id":"kc2","ch":"9","title":"Frank-Starling: more in, more out","core":"The Frank-Starling law says the heart pumps out whatever blood comes back to it. More venous return fills the ventricle more (higher EDV), which stretches the cardiac fibers closer to their best (optimal) length, so the next beat is stronger and SV rises. This is intrinsic control: it is built into the heart muscle and needs no nerve or hormone. It also keeps the right and left ventricles pumping the same amount.","trap":"Saying the sympathetic nerves \"sense the extra blood\" and order a stronger beat. That is extrinsic control. Frank-Starling is stretch alone: it raises SV by raising EDV, while sympathetic drive raises SV by lowering ESV.","q":"More blood comes back to the heart. Walk through Frank-Starling, and say whether it is intrinsic or extrinsic.","a":"More venous return, higher EDV, more stretch toward optimal length, stronger contraction, bigger SV. Intrinsic: no nerves or hormones needed."},{"id":"kc3","ch":"9","title":"The cardiac cycle and heart sounds","core":"One beat has four phases: filling (AV valves open), isovolumetric contraction (all four valves shut, pressure rising), ejection (semilunar valves open), and isovolumetric relaxation (all four valves shut, pressure falling). A valve opens or closes only because of a pressure difference; no valve opens on its own. S1, \"lub,\" is the AV valves closing at the start of systole, and S2, \"dub,\" is the semilunar valves closing at the end of systole. A murmur is a whooshing sound from turbulent flow through a valve that leaks (regurgitation) or does not open fully (stenosis).","trap":"Thinking a contracting ventricle must be ejecting. In isovolumetric contraction the ventricle squeezes, but every valve is shut, so volume stays the same until ventricular pressure beats aortic pressure. In isovolumetric relaxation every valve is also shut, but the pressure is falling.","q":"Name the four phases of one beat in order. Which valves are open in each, and where do S1 and S2 happen?","a":"Filling (AV valves open), isovolumetric contraction (all closed, begins with S1), ejection (semilunar valves open), isovolumetric relaxation (all closed, begins with S2). Then filling starts again."},{"id":"kc4","ch":"9","title":"The SA node is the pacemaker","core":"Several parts of the heart can fire on their own (autorhythmicity): the SA node at about 70 to 80 per minute, the AV node at 40 to 60, and the Purkinje fibers at 20 to 40. The SA node sets the beat simply because it is the fastest: it fires the rest of the heart before they reach threshold on their own. If the SA node fails, the AV node takes over at its slower rate. If the AV node fails too, the Purkinje fibers drive the ventricles even more slowly.","trap":"Saying the SA node is the ONLY tissue that can fire on its own. The AV node and Purkinje fibers are self-excitable too. The SA node wins on speed, not on being the only one.","q":"Give the three intrinsic rates. Why does the SA node win?","a":"SA node about 70 to 80 per minute, AV node 40 to 60, Purkinje fibers 20 to 40. The SA node is fastest, so it fires the rest of the heart before the slower sites can fire on their own."},{"id":"kc5","ch":"9","title":"Reading the ECG","core":"The ECG records the heart's electrical activity from the skin. The P wave is the atria depolarizing, and the atria contract right after it. The flat PR segment is the pause while the signal creeps through the AV node (about 0.1 second), so the atria can finish filling the ventricles. The QRS complex is the ventricles depolarizing (then they contract), and the T wave is the ventricles repolarizing (then they relax).","trap":"Mixing up the PR segment with the QRS complex or the T wave. The PR segment is the AV nodal delay, the QRS complex is ventricular depolarization, and the T wave is ventricular repolarization: not a delay and not a contraction.","q":"Say what each part of the ECG shows: P wave, PR segment, QRS complex, T wave.","a":"P wave: atrial depolarization. PR segment: the AV nodal delay. QRS complex: ventricular depolarization. T wave: ventricular repolarization."},{"id":"kc6","ch":"10","title":"MAP = CO × TPR","core":"Mean arterial pressure (MAP) is the average pressure that pushes blood into your tissues. It depends on just two things: MAP ≈ CO × TPR. Cardiac output (CO = HR × SV) is how much blood the heart pumps each minute, and total peripheral resistance (TPR) is how tightly the arterioles are squeezed. Raise either one and MAP rises; lower either one and MAP falls.","trap":"Saying \"MAP stays the same because the body compensates.\" The baroreflex will try to fix it later, but first give the direct effect: plug the change into the equation. If TPR falls and CO has not changed, MAP falls by the same fraction.","q":"Pump and pipes: which two quantities multiply to give MAP, and what does each one measure?","a":"MAP ≈ CO × TPR. CO (HR × SV) is the blood the heart pumps per minute; TPR is the resistance set mainly by the radius of the arterioles. Raise either one and MAP rises."},{"id":"kc7","ch":"10","title":"Flow follows the pressure difference","core":"Blood moves only when one end of a vessel pushes harder than the other. Flow = ΔP ÷ R, where ΔP is the pressure difference (the gradient) between the two ends and R is resistance. A high pressure at both ends with no difference means zero flow. For the whole body, the heart makes the gradient: about 93 mmHg in the aorta versus near 0 mmHg at the right atrium.","trap":"Picking the vessel with the biggest pressure number. Always subtract first: flow depends on the difference between the two ends, not on how high either number is.","q":"A garden hose is capped at the far end, so the water inside sits at the same high pressure everywhere. Why does no water move?","a":"Flow = ΔP ÷ R. With the same pressure at both ends, ΔP is 0, so flow is 0, no matter how high the pressure is. Blood vessels work the same way: the difference pushes, not the number."},{"id":"kc8","ch":"10","title":"Radius counts to the fourth power","core":"Resistance is the friction between blood and the vessel wall. It rises with blood thickness (viscosity) and vessel length, and it falls with the radius to the fourth power: R ∝ 1/r⁴. Viscosity and length barely change from day to day, but radius changes in seconds, and a small change in radius makes a huge change in resistance and flow. That is why the arterioles, with their adjustable smooth muscle, are the main resistance vessels.","trap":"Treating radius as a straight-line effect (\"double the radius, half the resistance\"). Raise the factor to the fourth power: 2⁴ = 16, so doubling the radius drops resistance to 1/16 and raises flow 16 times at the same gradient.","q":"Why does pinching a drinking straw just a little make it so much harder to sip through?","a":"Resistance goes with 1/r⁴. A small loss of radius is multiplied four times over, so resistance jumps far more than the pinch looks. Halving the radius would raise resistance 16 times and cut flow to 1/16."},{"id":"kc9","ch":"10","title":"The baroreflex in five steps","core":"The baroreflex is the body's fast, automatic fix for a change in blood pressure, and it works within seconds. Stretch receptors in the carotid sinus and aortic arch fire less when pressure falls (less stretch), and the medulla reads that drop in firing as \"pressure is low.\" The medulla turns sympathetic output up and parasympathetic output down, so heart rate and contractility rise, veins constrict and arterioles constrict. CO and TPR rise and MAP comes back up: detect, signal, process, act, result.","trap":"Saying baroreceptors fire MORE when pressure drops, \"to sound the alarm.\" They are stretch sensors, more like a speedometer than a smoke detector: less pressure, less stretch, less firing. The medulla reads the lower firing rate as low pressure.","q":"Say the five baroreflex steps out loud for a drop in pressure. At step 2, does receptor firing go up or down?","a":"Detect (stretch receptors in the carotid sinus and aortic arch), Signal (firing goes DOWN: less stretch), Process (medulla: sympathetic up, parasympathetic down), Act (HR up, contractility up, venoconstriction, arteriolar constriction), Result (CO and TPR up, MAP restored)."},{"id":"kc10","ch":"10","title":"Veins are the blood reservoir","core":"Veins have thin, stretchy walls, so they hold most of your blood (about 60 to 70 percent; Sherwood's figure shows 64 percent in the systemic veins) at low pressure. That makes them capacitance (storage) vessels, not resistance vessels. When sympathetic nerves constrict the veins, stored blood shifts toward the heart: venous return and end-diastolic volume (EDV) rise, and stroke volume rises by the Frank-Starling mechanism.","trap":"Saying venoconstriction raises total peripheral resistance \"the same way arteriolar constriction does.\" Veins store volume; arterioles set resistance. Squeezing veins raises MAP through CO (more filling, bigger SV), not through TPR.","q":"Your veins squeeze. Which part of MAP = CO × TPR goes up, and by what chain?","a":"CO. Stored blood moves to the heart, so venous return and EDV rise, and by Frank-Starling the stroke volume rises (CO = HR × SV). TPR barely changes, because veins are storage vessels, not resistance vessels."},{"id":"kc11","ch":"10","title":"Bulk flow, lymph and edema","core":"At a capillary, blood pressure pushes fluid out at the arteriolar end, and plasma proteins (which cannot leave) pull fluid back in at the venular end. More leaves than returns: about 20 L/day is filtered and only about 17 L/day is reabsorbed. The lymphatic vessels carry the extra 3 L/day back to the blood near the heart. If the lymph is blocked, or the balance of forces shifts, fluid builds up in the tissues as edema (swelling).","trap":"Thinking filtration and reabsorption balance exactly, so the lymph is \"just a backup.\" The surplus is normal and happens every day; the lymphatics are required to keep the tissues from swelling.","q":"Out, back, and the leftover: give the three daily fluid numbers at the capillaries and say where the leftover goes.","a":"About 20 L/day is filtered out, about 17 L/day is reabsorbed, and the extra 3 L/day is picked up by lymphatic vessels and returned to the venous blood near the heart. Block that path and the fluid stays in the tissue as edema."},{"id":"kc12","ch":"11","title":"The platelet plug: stick, activate, pile on","core":"When a blood vessel is cut, collagen in its wall is exposed. Adhesion: von Willebrand factor (vWF), a plasma protein, bridges platelets to that collagen. Activation: the stuck platelets change shape and release ADP and thromboxane A₂. Aggregation: those chemicals make passing platelets sticky, so they pile on and release still more, a positive feedback loop.","trap":"Naming \"activation\" as the broken step in von Willebrand disease. Activation fails only because adhesion failed first, so always name the earliest broken link. (Vascular spasm is the first step of hemostasis; adhesion is the first step of the plug.)","q":"Name the three steps of the platelet plug in order, with the key molecule for each.","a":"Adhesion: vWF bridges platelets to exposed collagen. Activation: stuck platelets change shape and release ADP and thromboxane A₂. Aggregation: more platelets pile on (positive feedback)."},{"id":"kc13","ch":"11","title":"Thrombin builds, plasmin removes","core":"The coagulation cascade is a chain of enzymes, each one switching on the next. It can start inside the blood (intrinsic) or from damaged tissue (extrinsic), and both doors lead into one common pathway that turns prothrombin into thrombin. Thrombin converts soluble fibrinogen into insoluble fibrin threads, a mesh that traps blood cells and locks the platelet plug in place. When the wall has healed, plasmin digests the fibrin and the clot dissolves (fibrinolysis).","trap":"Two mix-ups. (1) Thinking that blocking thrombin stops the platelet plug: the plug is upstream and separate, so it still forms but never gets its fibrin glue. (2) Swapping the enzymes: thrombin is the builder, plasmin is the demolisher, and fibrinogen is only the raw material.","q":"Thrombin or plasmin: which one builds the clot, which one tears it down, and what does each one act on?","a":"Thrombin builds: it turns soluble fibrinogen into insoluble fibrin. Plasmin removes: it digests the fibrin after the wall is repaired."},{"id":"kc14","ch":"11","title":"Anemia: fewer seats, not thinner air","core":"About 98.5% of the O₂ in blood rides on hemoglobin inside red cells; very little dissolves in plasma. Anemia (too few red cells or too little hemoglobin) lowers the total amount of O₂ the blood can carry. PO₂ measures only the dissolved O₂, which depends on the lungs, so it stays normal in anemia. Every seat on the bus is full; there are just fewer seats.","trap":"Assuming any O₂ delivery problem must show up as a low PO₂. Anemia is a capacity problem, not a pressure problem: PO₂ can be normal while the total O₂ content is dangerously low. PO₂ is not the same as O₂ content.","q":"Pressure or capacity: which one does anemia lower, and which one does a PO₂ reading measure?","a":"Anemia lowers capacity: less hemoglobin, so less total O₂ carried. PO₂ measures the pressure of dissolved O₂, set by the lungs, so it stays normal."},{"id":"kc15","ch":"11","title":"ABO blood types and transfusion","core":"Blood type is set by antigens (name tags) on the red cells: A, B, both (AB) or none (O). From early life, the plasma carries antibodies against the antigens you do NOT have: type A has anti-B, type B has anti-A, type O has both, and type AB has neither. In a transfusion reaction, the RECIPIENT's antibodies attack the DONOR's red cells, which clump (agglutinate) and burst (hemolysis).","trap":"Mixing up universal donor and universal recipient. Type O red cells can go to everyone because they carry no antigens, but a type O person can receive only O, because O plasma holds both anti-A and anti-B. \"Can give to everyone\" does not mean \"can receive from everyone.\"","q":"Type O blood: what is on the cells, what is in the plasma, and why is O the universal donor of red cells but the pickiest recipient?","a":"No A or B antigens on the cells; anti-A and anti-B in the plasma. With no antigens, O cells give a recipient's antibodies nothing to bind (universal donor). Its plasma attacks A, B and AB cells, so an O patient can receive only O."},{"id":"kc16","ch":"12","title":"Innate vs adaptive: the difference is memory","core":"Innate immunity is ready from birth: skin and mucus, phagocytes such as neutrophils and macrophages, NK cells, inflammation and complement. It acts within minutes to hours, recognizes broad patterns, and responds the same way every time. Adaptive immunity (B cells and T cells) targets one specific antigen and is slow the first time (days), but it remembers, so the next exposure brings a faster, stronger response.","trap":"Answering \"innate, because it is faster\" in a re-exposure story. Innate is faster on the first encounter, but it never improves. Protection that gets better the second time needs memory, and only the adaptive branch has memory.","q":"What single question sorts any defense into innate or adaptive?","a":"Does it get better the second time? Yes: adaptive (specific, memory, B and T cells). No: innate (fast, broad, the same every time)."},{"id":"kc17","ch":"12","title":"Plasma cells are the antibody factories","core":"Each B cell carries one antibody shape. When an antigen binds the matching B cell and a CD4 helper T cell gives the go-ahead, that B cell divides into a clone. Most of the clone become plasma cells: short-lived factories packed with rough endoplasmic reticulum that pour out antibody. A few become memory B cells, which live a long time and wait for the next exposure without secreting antibody.","trap":"Answering \"cytotoxic T cells\" (or memory B cells, or macrophages) when asked which cell secretes antibody. Cytotoxic T cells kill infected cells directly, and memory cells only wait. Antibody comes from the B cell line, and the actual factory is the plasma cell.","q":"An activated B cell has two kinds of daughter cells. Name them and say what each one does.","a":"Plasma cells: short-lived, full of rough ER, secrete large amounts of antibody. Memory B cells: long-lived, not secreting, ready to launch a faster response next time."},{"id":"kc18","ch":"12","title":"MHC and T cells: the 8 rule","core":"T cells cannot see free germs; they read pieces of antigen displayed on MHC molecules. Class I MHC is on every nucleated cell and shows pieces of proteins made inside it; CD8 cytotoxic T cells read class I and kill a cell that shows a foreign piece. Class II MHC is only on antigen-presenting cells (macrophages, dendritic cells, B cells) and shows pieces of what they swallowed; CD4 helper T cells read class II and then activate B cells and cytotoxic T cells.","trap":"The classic reversal: saying CD4 reads class I or CD8 reads class II. Check with the multiplication: CD8 × I = 8 and CD4 × II = 8. If a pairing gives 4 × 1 = 4, it is wrong.","q":"Say the MHC multiplication trick out loud, then give the job of each T cell it pairs.","a":"CD8 × I = 8: CD8 cytotoxic T cells read class I (on all nucleated cells) and kill. CD4 × II = 8: CD4 helper T cells read class II (on macrophages, dendritic cells, B cells) and activate B cells and cytotoxic T cells."},{"id":"kc19","ch":"13","title":"Boyle's law and inspiration","core":"Boyle's law: at a constant temperature, when the volume of a gas goes up, its pressure goes down (P₁V₁ = P₂V₂). To breathe in, the diaphragm and the external intercostals contract and make the chest bigger, and the lungs are pulled outward with it. The bigger lungs have a lower pressure inside: alveolar pressure falls from 760 to about 759 mmHg, about 1 mmHg below the outside air. Air flows in down that gradient until the two pressures are equal again.","trap":"The \"straw model\": saying the muscles suck air in. The muscles only change volume, Boyle's law changes the pressure, and the pressure gradient moves the air. Name all three links every time.","q":"Volume, pressure, gradient: say the three links that bring air into the lungs, and name the two muscles that start the chain.","a":"The diaphragm and external intercostals contract, so chest and lung volume go up. By Boyle's law alveolar pressure falls about 1 mmHg below atmospheric (760 to 759 mmHg). Air flows in down that gradient until alveolar pressure is back to 760."},{"id":"kc20","ch":"13","title":"Surfactant","core":"The inside of each alveolus is wet, and that thin water film pulls inward (surface tension), like a soap bubble trying to shrink. Small alveoli feel the strongest collapsing pull (law of LaPlace, P = 2T/r), so without help they would collapse and empty their air into bigger ones. Type II alveolar cells make surfactant, a lipid and protein mix that REDUCES surface tension. It works best in the small alveoli, where the same amount is packed onto a smaller surface, so every alveolus stays open and the lungs are easier to inflate.","trap":"Calling surfactant a mucus breaker (that is a mucolytic, and it acts on airway mucus) or saying it raises surface tension. Surfactant works inside the alveoli, and it LOWERS surface tension.","q":"Name the cell that makes surfactant, say what it does to surface tension, and tell what would happen to the tiniest alveoli without it.","a":"Type II alveolar cells make it. It lowers surface tension, most of all in the small alveoli, where surface tension would otherwise be highest. Without it the small alveoli would collapse and empty into larger ones."},{"id":"kc21","ch":"13","title":"Passive vs active expiration","core":"Breathing out at rest is passive: no muscle contracts. During the breath in, the lungs and chest wall were stretched like a rubber band, which stored energy. When the diaphragm and external intercostals relax, elastic recoil makes the lungs smaller, alveolar pressure rises to about 761 mmHg (above the 760 outside), and air flows out. Forced expiration (exercise, coughing, blowing out candles) is active: the abdominal muscles and the internal intercostals contract and use ATP.","trap":"Naming the abdominal muscles and internal intercostals as \"the muscles of expiration.\" They work only in forced expiration. For a quiet breath out at rest, the answer is no muscles at all: elastic recoil.","q":"You are sitting still and breathe out. Which muscles contract, and where does the energy come from? Then say what changes when you cough.","a":"None contract: the inspiratory muscles relax and the stretched lungs and chest wall recoil, using energy stored during inspiration (alveolar pressure about 761 mmHg). A cough is forced expiration: the abdominal muscles and internal intercostals contract, using ATP."},{"id":"kc22","ch":"13","title":"Restrictive vs obstructive lung disease","core":"Restrictive disease (pulmonary fibrosis is the model) means stiff, scarred lungs with low compliance: they cannot expand, so the problem is getting air IN, and total lung capacity and vital capacity are small. Obstructive disease (asthma, and COPD such as chronic bronchitis and emphysema) means narrowed airways: the problem is getting air OUT. Air gets trapped, so residual volume goes up while vital capacity and FEV₁ go down. Memory hook: restrictive cannot fill, obstructive cannot empty.","trap":"Hearing \"fibrosis\" and answering \"narrowed airways and trapped air.\" That describes obstructive disease. Fibrosis is restrictive: the airways are normal, the lung tissue is stiff, and the problem is getting air IN.","q":"Restrictive or obstructive: for each, give one example disease, the main problem (air in or air out), and what happens to the lung volumes.","a":"Restrictive: pulmonary fibrosis; stiff, scarred lungs; trouble getting air IN; total lung capacity and vital capacity are reduced. Obstructive: asthma or COPD; narrowed airways; trouble getting air OUT; air is trapped, so residual volume rises and FEV₁ falls."},{"id":"kc23","ch":"13","title":"Gas exchange by partial pressure gradients","core":"Each gas in a mixture has its own partial pressure, its share of the total (in room air, PO₂ = 0.21 × 760 ≈ 160 mmHg). In the lungs, O₂ moves from the alveoli (PO₂ about 100 to 104 mmHg) into the blood arriving from the body (PO₂ about 40), and CO₂ moves from that blood (PCO₂ about 46) into the alveoli (PCO₂ about 40). Each gas diffuses passively and on its own, down its own partial pressure gradient: no pump and no ATP. At the tissues the gradients point the other way, so O₂ leaves the blood and CO₂ enters it.","trap":"Saying the alveolar wall \"actively transports\" oxygen, or that the gases move by total pressure. Gas exchange is pure passive diffusion, and O₂ and CO₂ each follow their own gradient, even at the same moment and in opposite directions.","q":"Give the four lung numbers (alveolar air and arriving blood, for O₂ and for CO₂), then say which way each gas moves and what powers it.","a":"O₂: alveoli about 100 to 104 mmHg, arriving blood about 40, so O₂ moves into the blood. CO₂: arriving blood about 46, alveoli about 40, so CO₂ moves into the alveoli. Both move by passive diffusion down their own partial pressure gradients, with no ATP."},{"id":"kc24","ch":"13","title":"The O₂-hemoglobin curve: flat top, steep bottom","core":"The curve shows how full hemoglobin is (percent saturation) at each PO₂, and it is S-shaped, not a straight line. The flat top, from 60 to 100 mm Hg, is the lungs: at 100 hemoglobin is about 97.5% full, and at 60 it is still about 90%, a built-in safety margin for loading. The steep part, below about 60 mm Hg, is the tissues: a small drop in PO₂ makes hemoglobin let go of a lot of O₂ (at rest, tissue PO₂ is about 40 and hemoglobin is still 75% full; in hard exercise PO₂ can fall to about 20 and saturation to about a third).","trap":"Two mistakes: (1) reading the curve as a straight line, as if a big PO₂ drop on the flat top caused an equally big drop in saturation; (2) swapping the zones. Remember: FLAT = LOAD (lungs), STEEP = UNLOAD (tissues).","q":"Which part of the O₂ curve belongs to the lungs and which to the tissues, and what job does each part do?","a":"Flat top (60 to 100 mm Hg) = lungs: loading is protected, because saturation barely falls when PO₂ drops. Steep part (below about 60) = tissues: a small PO₂ drop unloads a lot of O₂."},{"id":"kc25","ch":"13","title":"Right shift = more unloading","core":"Some conditions loosen hemoglobin's grip on O₂ and shift the curve to the right: more CO₂, more acid (H⁺), more 2,3-DPG, exercise and a higher temperature (CADET, face Right). A right shift means LOWER affinity, so at the same tissue PO₂ hemoglobin releases MORE O₂. A working muscle makes heat, CO₂ and acid all at once, so it gets extra O₂ exactly where it is needed (the CO₂ and acid part is called the Bohr effect). The opposites (less CO₂, less acid, cold, fetal hemoglobin) shift the curve left: higher affinity, better loading, less unloading.","trap":"Reversing it: \"a right shift raises affinity, so hemoglobin holds on tighter.\" It is the opposite. Right = Release (lower affinity, more unloading). Left = Loads (higher affinity, holds tighter).","q":"Name the five CADET conditions. Which way do they push the curve, and does hemoglobin then hold O₂ tighter or looser?","a":"CO₂ up, Acid up, 2,3-DPG up, Exercise, Temperature up: the curve shifts RIGHT. Affinity falls, so hemoglobin holds looser and releases more O₂ at the same PO₂."},{"id":"kc26","ch":"13","title":"CO₂ rides home mostly as bicarbonate","core":"CO₂ travels back to the lungs three ways, and bicarbonate (HCO₃⁻) is the biggest: about 70% in our study guide (Sherwood rounds it to about 60%). Inside the red blood cell, the enzyme carbonic anhydrase joins CO₂ and water into carbonic acid, which splits into H⁺ and HCO₃⁻; the HCO₃⁻ leaves for the plasma while Cl⁻ comes in (the chloride shift). About 23% rides on hemoglobin as carbaminohemoglobin, stuck to the globin protein, and the last 7 to 10% is simply dissolved in plasma. At the lungs every step runs backward, and the CO₂ is breathed out.","trap":"Saying CO₂ binds the heme iron, the same seat O₂ uses. CO₂ on hemoglobin (carbaminohemoglobin) binds the globin protein chains, NOT the heme iron, so it is not fighting O₂ for the same site.","q":"Name the three ways CO₂ travels in the blood, biggest first, and say where CO₂ attaches on hemoglobin.","a":"Bicarbonate (about 70%, made in red cells by carbonic anhydrase, then the chloride shift), carbaminohemoglobin (about 23%, on the globin chains, not the heme iron), dissolved in plasma (about 7 to 10%)."},{"id":"kc27","ch":"13","title":"CO₂ drives breathing, sensed as H⁺","core":"Minute to minute, CO₂, not O₂, sets how hard you breathe. CO₂ crosses the blood-brain barrier easily (H⁺ cannot); in the brain's extracellular fluid it combines with water to make H⁺, and the central chemoreceptors in the medulla sense that H⁺ and turn breathing up. The O₂ sensors (peripheral chemoreceptors in the carotid and aortic bodies) are a backup that fires hard only when arterial PO₂ falls below about 60 mm Hg. Breathe too little and CO₂ and acid build up (respiratory acidosis); breathe too much and CO₂ falls and pH rises (respiratory alkalosis).","trap":"Two mistakes: (1) calling O₂ the main driver (it is a backup sensed by the peripheral chemoreceptors); (2) saying the central chemoreceptors detect CO₂ directly. They detect the H⁺ that CO₂ makes on the brain side of the barrier. CO₂ is the stimulus; H⁺ is the signal.","q":"What do the central chemoreceptors actually sense, where are they, and when do the O₂ sensors join in?","a":"They sit in the medulla and sense H⁺ in the brain's extracellular fluid, made when CO₂ crosses the blood-brain barrier and reacts with water. The peripheral O₂ sensors (carotid and aortic bodies) add a strong drive only when arterial PO₂ drops below about 60 mm Hg."},{"id":"kc28","ch":"13","title":"V/Q matching: in the lung, low O₂ squeezes","core":"V is ventilation (air reaching the alveoli) and Q is perfusion (blood flowing past them); gas exchange works only where the two meet. In body tissues, low O₂ dilates arterioles to bring in more blood. In the lung it is reversed: low alveolar PO₂ makes the local pulmonary arterioles CONSTRICT (hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction), steering blood away from poorly aired alveoli and toward well-aired ones, so blood does not pass through lung that cannot give it O₂.","trap":"Applying the body rule (\"low O₂ = vasodilate\") to the lung. In the lung, low alveolar O₂ means constrict, so blood goes where the air is. The study guide calls this reversal one of the most tested points in Chapter 13.","q":"Low O₂ next to a muscle arteriole versus low O₂ in an alveolus: what does each nearby arteriole do, and why does the lung do the opposite?","a":"Muscle arteriole: dilates, bringing more blood to tissue short of O₂. Pulmonary arteriole: constricts (hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction), sending blood to alveoli that have air, so perfusion matches ventilation."}],"mcq":[{"kc":"kc1","q":"At a sports physical, a long-distance swimmer's echo shows her left ventricle fills to 140 mL and still holds 40 mL after each squeeze. Her resting pulse is 50. How much blood does that ventricle pump in one minute?","c":["7,000 mL/min","2,000 mL/min","5,000 mL/min","100 mL/min"],"a":2,"why":"First SV = EDV − ESV = 140 − 40 = 100 mL. Then CO = HR × SV = 50 × 100 = 5,000 mL/min (5 L/min).","no":["That is HR × EDV (50 × 140). It counts the 40 mL that never leaves the ventricle.","That is HR × ESV. ESV is what stays behind, not what is pumped.","","100 mL is the stroke volume, one beat. Multiply by the heart rate to get the amount per minute."],"id":"q1"},{"kc":"kc1","q":"An echocardiogram shows a left ventricle that holds 110 mL when it is full and 45 mL right after it contracts. What is the stroke volume?","c":["65 mL","155 mL","110 mL","45 mL"],"a":0,"why":"SV = EDV − ESV = 110 − 45 = 65 mL, the volume actually pushed out in one beat.","no":["","That adds the two volumes. Stroke volume is the difference between them.","110 mL is the EDV, the full volume, not what left.","45 mL is the ESV, the blood that stays behind."],"id":"q2"},{"kc":"kc1","q":"Two patients both have a cardiac output of 6,000 mL/min. Patient X has a heart rate of 100, and Patient Y has a heart rate of 60. What are their stroke volumes?","c":["X 100 mL, Y 60 mL","Both 60 mL","Both 100 mL","X 60 mL, Y 100 mL"],"a":3,"why":"SV = CO ÷ HR. X: 6,000 ÷ 100 = 60 mL. Y: 6,000 ÷ 60 = 100 mL. The slower heart must push out more with each beat to move the same blood per minute.","no":["Reversed: the faster heart needs the smaller stroke volume.","Only X is 60 mL. Y beats less often, so each beat must be bigger.","Only Y is 100 mL. X beats more often, so each beat can be smaller.",""],"id":"q3"},{"kc":"kc1","q":"A drug makes the ventricle squeeze harder. EDV stays at 125 mL and the heart rate stays at 60, but the ESV drops from 55 mL to 35 mL. What happens to cardiac output?","c":["It stays at 7,500 mL/min, because EDV did not change","It rises from 4,200 to 5,400 mL/min","It falls, because a smaller ESV means less blood in the heart","It cannot rise unless the heart rate also rises"],"a":1,"why":"Before: SV = 125 − 55 = 70 mL, so CO = 60 × 70 = 4,200 mL/min. After: SV = 125 − 35 = 90 mL, so CO = 60 × 90 = 5,400 mL/min. Emptying more completely raises SV even with the same filling.","no":["7,500 is HR × EDV, the classic trap. EDV is not what is pumped out.","","A smaller ESV means more blood LEFT the ventricle, so SV goes up.","CO = HR × SV, so a bigger SV raises CO even at the same heart rate."],"id":"q4"},{"kc":"kc2","q":"A man lies flat and props his legs up on pillows, so more blood drains from his legs back to his heart. His nerve signals to the heart do not change. What happens to his next stroke volume, and why?","c":["It falls, because the ventricle is too full to contract well","It stays the same, because the heart cannot change its force without nerves","It rises, because the vagus nerve slows the heart and allows more filling","It rises, because the extra filling stretches the fibers toward their best length"],"a":3,"why":"More venous return raises EDV. The extra stretch moves the cardiac fibers closer to their optimal length, so they contract more forcefully and SV rises. That is the Frank-Starling law: intrinsic, no nerves needed.","no":["A normal heart works on the rising part of its length-tension curve: more filling gives more force, not less.","Changing force with no nerve input is exactly what Frank-Starling does.","The story says the nerve signals did not change. Frank-Starling needs no nerves at all.",""],"id":"q5"},{"kc":"kc2","q":"Which statement correctly contrasts the Frank-Starling mechanism with sympathetic stimulation of the ventricles?","c":["Frank-Starling raises SV by raising EDV; sympathetic stimulation raises SV mainly by lowering ESV","Both need norepinephrine from sympathetic nerves","Frank-Starling lowers ESV; sympathetic stimulation raises EDV","Frank-Starling works only during exercise; sympathetic stimulation works only at rest"],"a":0,"why":"Frank-Starling is stretch from more filling (higher EDV). Sympathetic drive adds Ca²⁺ and makes each beat stronger at any filling, so the ventricle empties more completely (lower ESV).","no":["","Frank-Starling is intrinsic: it needs no nerve or hormone.","Reversed: filling (EDV) is the Frank-Starling side, emptying (ESV) is the sympathetic side.","Frank-Starling works on every beat, at rest and during exercise."],"id":"q6"},{"kc":"kc2","q":"For a few beats, the right ventricle pumps slightly more blood than the left ventricle. What brings their outputs back into balance?","c":["The vagus nerve slows only the right ventricle","The SA node fires twice for the left ventricle","The extra blood reaches the left ventricle, raises its EDV, and by Frank-Starling it pumps more","The adrenal glands release epinephrine that acts only on the left ventricle"],"a":2,"why":"Extra output from the right side means extra blood arriving at the left side a few beats later. The higher left EDV stretches its fibers and raises its SV automatically, so the two outputs match.","no":["The vagus mainly slows the SA node, which sets the rate for both ventricles together.","One SA node impulse drives both ventricles at the same time.","","Epinephrine reaches the whole heart. It is extrinsic, and it is not what matches the two sides."],"id":"q7"},{"kc":"kc3","q":"A nurse listens to a patient's chest with a stethoscope. Which event makes the first heart sound, S1 (\"lub\")?","c":["The AV valves closing as the ventricles begin to contract","The semilunar valves closing at the end of systole","The AV valves opening as filling begins","The atria contracting"],"a":0,"why":"S1 is the AV (tricuspid and mitral) valves closing at the start of ventricular systole. A valve opening makes no sound.","no":["","That is S2, \"dub.\"","Valves opening are silent; the sounds come from valves closing.","Atrial contraction tops off filling, but it is not what makes S1."],"id":"q8"},{"kc":"kc3","q":"On a volume tracing, the left ventricle holds steady at 125 mL while its pressure climbs from about 10 to 80 mmHg. A moment later the volume begins to drop. What event ended the flat stretch?","c":["The papillary muscles pulled the AV valves open","The SA node fired again","Ventricular pressure fell below atrial pressure","Ventricular pressure rose above aortic pressure and pushed the aortic valve open"],"a":3,"why":"The flat stretch is isovolumetric contraction. Once ventricular pressure is higher than aortic pressure (about 80 mmHg), the aortic semilunar valve is pushed open and ejection begins, so volume falls.","no":["Papillary muscles only keep the AV flaps from flipping backward, and open AV valves would let volume rise.","The SA node fired earlier and started this beat. It does not open valves.","That opens the AV valves at the end of relaxation, and volume would rise, not fall.",""],"id":"q9"},{"kc":"kc3","q":"A doctor hears a whooshing murmur between S1 and S2, loudest over the aortic valve. The valve cusps are stiff and do not open fully. What is this?","c":["Aortic regurgitation: blood leaks backward into the ventricle","Aortic stenosis: blood is forced through a narrowed opening during ejection","A normal sound of blood filling the ventricle","The AV valves closing late"],"a":1,"why":"A valve that does not open fully is stenotic. Blood squeezes through the narrow opening during ejection, which falls between S1 and S2, and the turbulent flow makes the murmur.","no":["Regurgitation is a leak when the valve should be shut. For the aortic valve that happens during relaxation and filling, after S2.","","Normal flow is quiet. A whoosh means turbulent flow.","AV valve closing is S1, a normal sound, not a whoosh."],"id":"q10"},{"kc":"kc3","q":"Right after the second heart sound, the relaxing ventricle holds about 50 mL and its volume stays there for a moment. What lets it start to refill?","c":["The semilunar valves reopen and blood flows back in from the aorta","The ventricle contracts again to suck blood in","Ventricular pressure drops below atrial pressure, so the AV valves are pushed open","The papillary muscles pull the AV valves open"],"a":2,"why":"After S2 the ventricle is in isovolumetric relaxation: all valves shut, volume constant, pressure falling. When ventricular pressure drops below atrial pressure, the AV valves are pushed open and filling begins.","no":["The semilunar valves just closed (that was S2) and stay shut until the next ejection.","Contracting raises pressure. Filling happens while the ventricle relaxes.","","Papillary muscles only hold the AV flaps so they do not flip backward. Pressure opens the valves."],"id":"q11"},{"kc":"kc4","q":"A patient's AV node is destroyed, so no signal from the atria can reach the ventricles. His atria still beat at 75 per minute. About how fast will his ventricles beat?","c":["About 20 to 40 per minute, set by the Purkinje fibers","About 75 per minute, set by the SA node","Zero: they stop, because only the SA node can fire on its own","About 120 per minute, to make up for the block"],"a":0,"why":"The SA node still drives the atria, but its signal cannot cross to the ventricles. The fastest tissue left below the block, the Purkinje fibers, takes over the ventricles at its own slow rate of about 20 to 40 per minute.","no":["","The SA signal cannot cross; that rate stays in the atria only.","The trap: the Purkinje fibers are self-excitable too, so the ventricles keep beating.","Backup pacemakers are slower than the SA node, not faster."],"id":"q12"},{"kc":"kc4","q":"In a research lab, a small piece of living AV node tissue is kept in a warm dish with no nerves attached and no SA node nearby. What will it do?","c":["Stay silent, because only the SA node can fire on its own","Fire at about 70 to 80 per minute, like the SA node","Fire only when a nerve stimulates it","Fire on its own at about 40 to 60 per minute"],"a":3,"why":"The AV node is autorhythmic: it drifts to threshold by itself. With no faster SA node firing it first, it shows its own slower rate of about 40 to 60 per minute.","no":["The trap: the AV node and Purkinje fibers can fire on their own too.","70 to 80 is the SA node's rate. The AV node is slower.","Pacemaker cells need no nerve to fire. Nerves only speed them up or slow them down.",""],"id":"q13"},{"kc":"kc4","q":"A disease damages only the SA node, so it now fires just 30 times a minute. The AV node and Purkinje fibers are healthy. About how fast will the heart beat?","c":["30 per minute, because the SA node is always the pacemaker","40 to 60 per minute, because the AV node now reaches threshold first","70 to 80 per minute, because the AV node speeds up to replace it","20 to 40 per minute, because the Purkinje fibers take over first"],"a":1,"why":"The pacemaker is whoever reaches threshold first. At 30 per minute the sick SA node is now slower than the AV node (40 to 60), so the AV node fires first and sets the rate.","no":["The SA node leads only because it is normally the fastest. Once it is slower, it loses.","","Each site keeps its own intrinsic rate. The AV node does not speed up to the SA rate.","The AV node (40 to 60) is faster than the Purkinje fibers, so it takes over before they do."],"id":"q14"},{"kc":"kc5","q":"On an ECG strip, which part comes just before the ventricles start to contract?","c":["P wave","T wave","QRS complex","PR segment"],"a":2,"why":"The QRS complex is ventricular depolarization. Electrical events come just before the mechanical ones, so the ventricles contract right after the QRS.","no":["The P wave is atrial depolarization. It comes before the atria contract.","The T wave is ventricular repolarization, before the ventricles relax.","","The PR segment is the AV nodal delay, while the atria finish contracting."],"id":"q15"},{"kc":"kc5","q":"A patient's ECG shows a PR segment much longer than normal, but every P wave is still followed by a QRS complex. Where is the signal being slowed?","c":["In the ventricles, as they repolarize","In the AV node","In the SA node, before the P wave","In the atria, during the P wave"],"a":1,"why":"The PR segment is the AV nodal delay. A longer PR segment means the signal is creeping through the AV node even more slowly than normal before it reaches the ventricles.","no":["Ventricular repolarization is the T wave, after the QRS.","","A slow SA node would change how often P waves appear, not the gap after each one.","Atrial depolarization is the P wave itself, which comes before the PR segment."],"id":"q16"},{"kc":"kc5","q":"A student labels the T wave \"the ventricles contracting.\" What is the best correction?","c":["That is right: the T wave is ventricular contraction","The T wave is the AV nodal delay","The T wave is atrial repolarization","The T wave is ventricular repolarization, and the ventricles relax right after it"],"a":3,"why":"The ECG shows electrical events, not squeezing. The T wave is the ventricles repolarizing, which is followed by relaxation. Contraction follows the QRS complex.","no":["ECG waves are electrical. The ventricles contract after the QRS, not at the T wave.","The AV nodal delay is the PR segment.","Atrial repolarization happens during the QRS complex and is hidden by it.",""],"id":"q17"},{"kc":"kc6","q":"A patient's cardiac output is 6 L/min and her total peripheral resistance is 15 mmHg per (L/min). Using MAP ≈ CO × TPR, what is her mean arterial pressure?","c":["About 90 mmHg","About 21 mmHg","About 2.5 mmHg","About 9 mmHg"],"a":0,"why":"MAP ≈ CO × TPR = 6 × 15 = 90 mmHg, a normal mean pressure.","no":["","That adds CO and TPR (6 + 15); the equation multiplies them.","That divides TPR by CO (15 ÷ 6); MAP is the product.","That is 15 minus 6; MAP is CO times TPR."],"id":"q18"},{"kc":"kc6","q":"A drug constricts arterioles all over the body and raises TPR by 50 percent. In the first moments, before any reflex responds, cardiac output has not changed. What happens to MAP?","c":["It stays the same, because the baroreflex cancels the change","It falls, because narrow arterioles let less blood through","It rises by about 50 percent","It doubles, because resistance counts to the fourth power"],"a":2,"why":"MAP ≈ CO × TPR. CO is unchanged and TPR is 1.5 times normal, so MAP is about 1.5 times normal. Give the direct effect first; the reflex comes after.","no":["The baroreflex reacts after the change; the question asks for the direct effect first.","Less flow through those arterioles is true, but the pressure upstream of them rises.","","The fourth power links radius to resistance. Here TPR itself is given as up 50 percent, and MAP follows TPR one to one."],"id":"q19"},{"kc":"kc6","q":"A beta blocker slows a man's heart from 80 to 60 beats/min. His stroke volume and TPR stay the same. Which statement about his MAP is correct?","c":["MAP rises, because a slower heart fills more","MAP is unchanged, because heart rate is not in the MAP equation","MAP falls to about half","MAP falls to about three quarters of what it was"],"a":3,"why":"CO = HR × SV, so CO falls to 60/80 = 3/4 of before. With TPR unchanged, MAP ≈ CO × TPR also falls to about 3/4.","no":["The question holds stroke volume constant, so a slower heart pumps less blood per minute.","Heart rate is inside CO (CO = HR × SV), and CO is in the MAP equation.","Heart rate fell by one quarter, not by half.",""],"id":"q20"},{"kc":"kc6","q":"Diastole lasts about twice as long as systole, so the mean pressure sits closer to the bottom number. For a reading of 135 over 75 mmHg, which estimate of MAP is best?","c":["105 mmHg","95 mmHg","60 mmHg","115 mmHg"],"a":1,"why":"Pulse pressure = 135 minus 75 = 60. MAP ≈ diastolic + one third of pulse pressure = 75 + 20 = 95 mmHg.","no":["That is the plain average of 135 and 75; it ignores that diastole lasts longer.","","That is the pulse pressure (135 minus 75), not the mean.","That adds two thirds of the pulse pressure; the rule is diastolic plus one third."],"id":"q21"},{"kc":"kc7","q":"An artery segment has 90 mmHg at its upstream end and 30 mmHg at its downstream end. Its resistance is 3 mmHg per (mL/min). What is the flow through it?","c":["30 mL/min","40 mL/min","20 mL/min","180 mL/min"],"a":2,"why":"Flow = ΔP ÷ R. ΔP = 90 minus 30 = 60 mmHg, and 60 ÷ 3 = 20 mL/min.","no":["That uses only the upstream pressure (90 ÷ 3); flow depends on the difference.","That adds the two pressures (120 ÷ 3); subtract them instead.","","That multiplies the gradient by resistance (60 × 3); flow is the gradient divided by resistance."],"id":"q22"},{"kc":"kc7","q":"Vessel X reads 160 mmHg at its inlet and 150 mmHg at its outlet. Vessel Y reads 50 mmHg at its inlet and 10 mmHg at its outlet. Both have the same resistance. Which one carries more blood per minute?","c":["Vessel X, because its pressures are much higher","Both the same, because the resistance is the same","It cannot be known without the radius of each vessel","Vessel Y, because its pressure difference is 40 mmHg versus 10 mmHg"],"a":3,"why":"Flow = ΔP ÷ R. X has a gradient of 10 mmHg; Y has 40 mmHg. With the same R, Y carries four times the flow.","no":["High absolute pressure does not drive flow; only the difference between the ends does.","Same resistance, but the gradients differ (10 versus 40), so the flows differ.","Radius matters only through resistance, and the resistances are already equal.",""],"id":"q23"},{"kc":"kc7","q":"A small artery runs from 70 mmHg at one end to 30 mmHg at the other. A medication raises the pressure at both ends by 25 mmHg, to 95 and 55. Resistance does not change. What happens to flow through the artery?","c":["It stays the same, because the gradient is still 40 mmHg","It rises, because both pressures went up","It falls, because the outlet pressure rose","It rises by 25 percent"],"a":0,"why":"Flow = ΔP ÷ R. Before: 70 minus 30 = 40 mmHg. After: 95 minus 55 = 40 mmHg. Same gradient and same R, so the same flow.","no":["","Higher numbers at both ends do not matter if the difference is unchanged.","The inlet rose by the same amount, so the difference is still 40.","There is no 25 percent in the math: the gradient is 40 before and after."],"id":"q24"},{"kc":"kc8","q":"Strong sympathetic firing squeezes an arteriole in the skin until its radius is half of what it was. The pressure gradient across it does not change. Flow through that arteriole becomes:","c":["one half of before","one quarter of before","one sixteenth of before","twice what it was"],"a":2,"why":"R ∝ 1/r⁴. Half the radius gives (1/2)⁴ = 1/16, so resistance rises 16 times and, at the same gradient, flow falls to 1/16.","no":["That treats radius as a straight-line effect; it counts to the fourth power.","That uses the square (2² = 4) instead of the fourth power.","","Narrowing raises resistance, so flow falls, not rises."],"id":"q25"},{"kc":"kc8","q":"During a run, local signals (low O₂, more CO₂ and adenosine) relax the arterioles in the calf until each radius is 1.5 times larger. The pressure gradient is unchanged. About how much does flow through those arterioles increase?","c":["About 1.5 times","About 2.25 times","About 5 times","About 6 times"],"a":2,"why":"1.5⁴ = 1.5 × 1.5 × 1.5 × 1.5 ≈ 5. Resistance falls to about 1/5, so flow rises about 5 times.","no":["That treats radius as a straight-line effect.","That is 1.5 squared; the rule uses the fourth power.","","That multiplies 1.5 by 4; the radius factor is raised to the 4th power, not multiplied by 4."],"id":"q26"},{"kc":"kc8","q":"Patient P has blood twice as viscous as normal (too many red cells). Patient Q has normal blood, but one of his vessels has its radius cut in half. Compared with normal, whose vessel resistance is higher, and by how much?","c":["P, because viscosity counts to the fourth power","Q: halving the radius raises resistance 16 times, while doubling viscosity only doubles it","They are equal, because both changes are a factor of 2","P, because thick blood also narrows the vessel"],"a":1,"why":"Resistance is proportional to viscosity (×2 gives ×2) but to 1/r⁴ (half the radius gives ×16). Radius is by far the most powerful factor.","no":["Viscosity is a straight-line factor; only radius has the fourth power.","","Both are a factor of 2, but radius is raised to the 4th power: 2 versus 16.","Viscosity does not change the vessel radius."],"id":"q27"},{"kc":"kc9","q":"Where are the arterial baroreceptors that run the baroreflex?","c":["In the carotid bodies and aortic bodies","In the cardiovascular control center of the medulla","In the walls of the arterioles they control","In the carotid sinus and the aortic arch"],"a":3,"why":"The baroreceptors are stretch receptors in the carotid sinus (at the fork of each carotid artery) and the aortic arch. They send their signals to the medulla.","no":["The carotid and aortic bodies are chemoreceptors that sense blood O₂, CO₂ and acid, not stretch.","The medulla is the integrating center, not the sensor.","The arterioles are effectors that respond to the reflex; they do not sense pressure for it.",""],"id":"q28"},{"kc":"kc9","q":"Which statement best describes how carotid sinus baroreceptors behave?","c":["They stay silent at normal pressure and fire only when pressure is too low","They fire faster whenever pressure falls, to warn the medulla","They fire all the time, and their rate goes up and down with how much the artery wall is stretched","They fire in step with heart rate, whatever the pressure"],"a":2,"why":"Baroreceptors are stretch sensors that fire continuously. More pressure stretches the wall more and raises the rate; less pressure lowers it. The medulla reads the rate.","no":["They fire continuously at normal pressure; that steady rate lets them signal both rises and falls.","That is the classic reversal: less pressure means less stretch, so they fire LESS.","","They follow wall stretch (pressure), not heart rate itself."],"id":"q29"},{"kc":"kc9","q":"To stop a racing heart rhythm, a doctor firmly massages the carotid sinus on one side of a patient's neck. The baroreceptors there feel extra stretch. What does the reflex do?","c":["The medulla reads \"pressure is high,\" raises parasympathetic (vagal) output and lowers sympathetic output, so the heart slows","The medulla reads \"pressure is low\" and raises sympathetic output, so the heart speeds up","The baroreceptors release acetylcholine straight onto the SA node","Nothing, because the baroreflex only responds to falling pressure"],"a":0,"why":"Extra stretch makes the baroreceptors fire more, which the medulla reads as high pressure. It answers as it would for a real rise: parasympathetic up, sympathetic down, and the heart rate falls.","no":["","Extra stretch means more firing, which signals high pressure, not low.","Baroreceptors are sensors; the vagus nerve, not the receptor, releases ACh at the SA node.","The reflex runs both ways; more stretch drives the reverse response."],"id":"q30"},{"kc":"kc9","q":"Which response to a fall in blood pressure is NOT one of the four fast baroreflex actions that work within seconds?","c":["Arterioles constrict","The kidneys hold on to more salt and water over hours","Veins constrict","Ventricular contractility increases"],"a":1,"why":"The four fast sympathetic actions are heart rate up, contractility up, venoconstriction and arteriolar constriction. Kidney salt and water retention (renin, angiotensin, aldosterone) is a slower response covered in the kidney chapters.","no":["Arteriolar constriction is one of the four; it raises TPR.","","Venoconstriction is one of the four; it raises venous return and SV.","Higher contractility is one of the four; it raises SV and CO."],"id":"q31"},{"kc":"kc10","q":"At rest, where is most of your blood at any moment?","c":["In the systemic arteries","In the capillaries","In the heart chambers","In the systemic veins"],"a":3,"why":"Veins hold about 60 to 70 percent of the blood (Sherwood's figure shows 64 percent in the systemic veins). Their thin, stretchy walls hold large volumes at low pressure.","no":["Systemic arteries hold only about 13 percent; they store pressure, not volume.","Capillaries hold only about 5 percent at any moment, even though they are the exchange site.","The heart holds only about 7 percent.",""],"id":"q32"},{"kc":"kc10","q":"Drug 1 constricts only arterioles. Drug 2 constricts only veins. In MAP ≈ CO × TPR, which term does each drug mainly raise?","c":["Both mainly raise TPR","Drug 1 raises TPR; Drug 2 raises CO by sending more blood back to fill the heart","Drug 1 raises CO; Drug 2 raises TPR","Neither raises MAP, because the baroreflex cancels both"],"a":1,"why":"Arterioles are resistance vessels, so constricting them raises TPR. Veins are storage vessels: constricting them raises venous return and EDV, so SV and CO rise by Frank-Starling.","no":["That is the classic trap: veins store volume; they are not resistance vessels.","","That reverses the two vessel jobs.","The question asks for the direct effect; both drugs raise MAP, through different terms."],"id":"q33"},{"kc":"kc10","q":"Nitroglycerin, a drug for chest pain, mainly relaxes (dilates) the veins. Which chain explains why it lowers the heart's workload?","c":["More blood stays in the veins, so venous return and EDV fall, so stroke volume falls by Frank-Starling","TPR falls sharply because veins are the main resistance vessels","More blood is pushed to the heart, so the heart fills more and works less","The veins absorb oxygen, so the heart needs less"],"a":0,"why":"Relaxed veins hold more blood, the opposite of venoconstriction. Less blood returns, the ventricle fills less (lower EDV), and by Frank-Starling it ejects a smaller stroke volume with less work.","no":["","Veins are storage vessels; dilating them shifts volume, it does not cut TPR much.","That is what venoconstriction does; dilated veins keep blood away from the heart.","Veins do not absorb oxygen; the effect is about where the blood volume sits."],"id":"q34"},{"kc":"kc11","q":"Capillaries filter about 20 L of fluid a day and reabsorb about 17 L. If lymph flow stopped completely for one day, about how much extra fluid would be left in the tissues?","c":["None, because filtration and reabsorption balance","About 17 L","About 37 L","About 3 L"],"a":3,"why":"20 minus 17 = 3 L/day. The lymphatics normally return this surplus to the blood; with no lymph flow, it stays in the interstitial fluid.","no":["They do not balance; filtration exceeds reabsorption every day.","That is the amount reabsorbed, which still returns to the blood.","That adds the two numbers; the leftover is the difference.",""],"id":"q35"},{"kc":"kc11","q":"At the arteriolar end of a capillary, capillary blood pressure is 40 mmHg, plasma colloid osmotic pressure is 25 mmHg, interstitial fluid hydrostatic pressure is 1 mmHg, and interstitial colloid osmotic pressure is 0. What is the net pressure, and which way does fluid move?","c":["14 mmHg, out of the capillary","14 mmHg, into the capillary","16 mmHg, out of the capillary","66 mmHg, out of the capillary"],"a":0,"why":"Outward: 40 + 0 = 40 mmHg. Inward: 25 + 1 = 26 mmHg. Net = 40 minus 26 = 14 mmHg outward, so fluid is filtered out.","no":["","The outward forces win here (40 versus 26), so fluid leaves.","That treats interstitial hydrostatic pressure as pushing out; it pushes in.","That adds every force; the inward forces must be subtracted."],"id":"q36"},{"kc":"kc11","q":"A child with a kidney disease loses large amounts of albumin, the main plasma protein, in his urine. His face and legs become puffy. Which force changed?","c":["Capillary blood pressure rose","Plasma colloid osmotic pressure fell, so less fluid is pulled back in at the venular end","The lymphatic vessels started filtering fluid out","Interstitial proteins were pumped into the blood"],"a":1,"why":"Plasma proteins, mostly albumin, create the inward pull (about 25 mmHg). Less albumin means less pull, so less fluid returns; the surplus outruns the lymph and collects as edema.","no":["Nothing in the story raises capillary blood pressure; the change is in plasma protein.","","Lymphatic vessels collect fluid; they do not filter it out.","That would raise the inward pull and reduce swelling, not cause it."],"id":"q37"},{"kc":"kc12","q":"In a lab test, a patient's platelets in her own plasma are washed over a collagen-coated slide, and they roll past without sticking. When a little plasma from a healthy donor is added, they stick at once. What did the healthy plasma supply?","c":["Thromboxane A₂","Von Willebrand factor","Fibrinogen","Plasmin"],"a":1,"why":"Sticking to collagen is adhesion, and the bridge protein for adhesion is von Willebrand factor, which circulates in plasma. Her platelets were fine; her plasma lacked the bridge.","no":["Thromboxane A₂ is released by platelets after they stick (activation); it cannot make them stick in the first place.","","Fibrinogen is the raw material for the fibrin mesh, a later step in a separate pathway.","Plasmin digests fibrin to remove a clot; it never helps platelets stick."],"id":"q38"},{"kc":"kc12","q":"A man has a normal von Willebrand factor level, but his platelets are missing the surface receptor that grabs it. In a test tube, his platelets change shape and release ADP normally when stimulated. At a small cut, which step of the platelet plug fails first?","c":["Activation, because his platelets cannot release ADP","Aggregation, because thromboxane A₂ is missing","Fibrin formation, because thrombin cannot be made","Adhesion, because his platelets cannot attach to the vWF bridge on the collagen"],"a":3,"why":"The bridge is there, but his platelets have no hand to grab it, so they never stick to the collagen. Adhesion is the earliest broken link; activation and aggregation fail only as a result.","no":["The test tube shows his platelets activate normally; at the cut they fail only because they never stuck.","Nothing suggests thromboxane is missing, and aggregation is downstream of the real break.","The coagulation cascade is a separate pathway, and thrombin can still be made.",""],"id":"q39"},{"kc":"kc12","q":"Aspirin blocks the enzyme platelets use to make thromboxane A₂. A man who takes a daily aspirin nicks himself shaving. What happens to his platelet plug?","c":["Platelets still stick to the collagen, but fewer new platelets are recruited to pile on","Platelets cannot stick to the collagen at all","The plug forms normally, but plasmin dissolves it at once","No fibrin can form, because thrombin is blocked"],"a":0,"why":"Adhesion through vWF still works. But the stuck platelets release less thromboxane A₂, so the positive feedback that recruits more platelets (aggregation) is weaker and the plug is smaller.","no":["","Adhesion depends on von Willebrand factor and collagen, which aspirin does not touch.","Aspirin does not turn on plasmin.","Aspirin works on platelets, not on thrombin or the cascade."],"id":"q40"},{"kc":"kc13","q":"A technician adds a few drops of thrombin to a tube of plasma that has no cells in it. Within seconds the liquid sets into a gel. Which plasma protein was converted to make the gel?","c":["Prothrombin, into thrombin","Plasminogen, into plasmin","Fibrinogen, into fibrin","Von Willebrand factor, into collagen"],"a":2,"why":"Thrombin's job is to turn soluble fibrinogen into insoluble fibrin threads. The threads form a mesh, so the liquid sets into a gel.","no":["Prothrombin is what gets turned INTO thrombin; here the thrombin was added ready-made.","Plasminogen becomes plasmin, which dissolves fibrin; that would never make a gel.","","vWF is a bridge for platelets and never becomes collagen, which belongs to the vessel wall."],"id":"q41"},{"kc":"kc13","q":"A newborn has very little vitamin K, which the liver needs to make prothrombin and several other clotting factors. He oozes blood from his umbilical cord stump, and his platelet count is normal. Which part of hemostasis is weakest?","c":["Platelet adhesion to collagen","Making thrombin, so too little fibrin forms","Plasmin removing old clots","Recruiting platelets with thromboxane A₂"],"a":1,"why":"Without vitamin K the liver makes too little prothrombin, so little thrombin forms and little fibrin is laid down. His platelets can plug the cut, but the plug is never locked in by a fibrin mesh.","no":["Adhesion needs vWF and collagen, not vitamin K.","","Weak plasmin would leave too much clot, not cause oozing.","Thromboxane A₂ is made by platelets, and his platelets are normal."],"id":"q42"},{"kc":"kc13","q":"A tiny wound starts the coagulation cascade with only a few activated factor molecules, yet within minutes there is enough fibrin to seal it. Why?","c":["The liver makes fibrinogen faster during bleeding","Plasmin switches off during an injury","Platelets turn into fibrin as they pile on","Each activated enzyme activates many molecules of the next, and thrombin speeds up its own production"],"a":3,"why":"A cascade amplifies: one enzyme turns on many copies of the next, step after step. Thrombin also feeds back to boost the cascade (positive feedback), so a small trigger becomes a lot of fibrin.","no":["Fibrinogen is already circulating in large amounts; the speed comes from the enzyme chain.","Plasmin is trapped in the clot and acts later; it is not what makes clotting fast.","Platelets are cell fragments; fibrin comes from fibrinogen, not from platelets.",""],"id":"q43"},{"kc":"kc13","q":"A stroke patient arrives with a clot already blocking an artery in her brain. The team gives tPA (tissue plasminogen activator). How does tPA clear the artery?","c":["It turns plasminogen into plasmin, which digests the fibrin mesh","It blocks thromboxane A₂, so platelets stop piling on","It blocks vitamin K, so the liver makes fewer clotting factors","It helps thrombin turn fibrin back into fibrinogen"],"a":0,"why":"tPA is a clot buster: it activates plasmin, the enzyme that removes clots by digesting fibrin. Thrombin builds, plasmin removes.","no":["","That is aspirin: it limits new platelet build-up but cannot dissolve a clot that is already there.","That is warfarin: it slows new clotting over days and does not dissolve an existing clot.","Thrombin only builds fibrin; nothing turns fibrin back into fibrinogen."],"id":"q44"},{"kc":"kc14","q":"Where is almost all of the O₂ in a sample of arterial blood found?","c":["Dissolved in the plasma","Bound to albumin","Bound to hemoglobin inside red blood cells","Carried as bicarbonate"],"a":2,"why":"O₂ dissolves poorly in plasma, so about 98.5% rides on hemoglobin. That is why losing hemoglobin cuts the blood's O₂ supply so badly.","no":["Only about 1.5% is dissolved, and that small part is what PO₂ measures.","Albumin carries many substances, but not O₂.","","Bicarbonate is how most CO₂ travels, not O₂."],"id":"q45"},{"kc":"kc14","q":"A woman on dialysis for kidney failure is pale and gets tired walking to her mailbox. Her lungs are healthy. Which set of lab results fits her best?","c":["Low hemoglobin, normal arterial PO₂","Normal hemoglobin, low arterial PO₂","High hematocrit, low arterial PO₂","High hemoglobin, high arterial PO₂"],"a":0,"why":"Failed kidneys make little erythropoietin (EPO), so the marrow makes fewer red cells: low hemoglobin and low O₂-carrying capacity. Her healthy lungs keep the dissolved O₂, and so the PO₂, normal.","no":["","A low PO₂ points to a lung or altitude problem; hers is too few red cells.","Without EPO the hematocrit falls, it does not rise.","With little EPO her hemoglobin is low, and PO₂ does not climb above normal on room air."],"id":"q46"},{"kc":"kc14","q":"Two people feel short of breath. Ana is camping at 4,300 m (about 14,000 ft) and has normal hemoglobin. Ben is at sea level and has iron-deficiency anemia. Which statement is correct?","c":["Both have a low arterial PO₂","Ana has a low arterial PO₂; Ben has a normal PO₂ but carries less total O₂","Ben has a low arterial PO₂; Ana has a normal PO₂","Neither has a real O₂ problem, because both PO₂ values are normal"],"a":1,"why":"Thin air lowers the pressure of O₂ entering Ana's blood, so her PO₂ falls: a pressure problem. Ben's lungs load O₂ normally, so his PO₂ is normal, but with less hemoglobin his blood holds less O₂: a capacity problem.","no":["Ben breathes normal air with normal lungs, so his PO₂ is normal.","","Reversed: altitude lowers PO₂; anemia does not.","Ana's PO₂ is low, and Ben's normal PO₂ hides a real shortage of O₂ content."],"id":"q47"},{"kc":"kc15","q":"A lab mixes a patient's red cells with anti-A serum in one well and anti-B serum in another. The cells clump only in the anti-B well. What is the patient's blood type, and which antibody is in the patient's plasma?","c":["Type A; anti-B in the plasma","Type AB; no antibodies in the plasma","Type B; anti-A in the plasma","Type O; anti-A and anti-B in the plasma"],"a":2,"why":"Clumping with anti-B means the cells carry the B antigen and not A: type B. People carry antibodies against the antigen they lack, so type B plasma holds anti-A.","no":["Type A cells would clump with anti-A, not anti-B.","Type AB cells would clump in both wells.","","Type O cells have no A or B antigen, so they would clump in neither well."],"id":"q48"},{"kc":"kc15","q":"A type B man is given a unit of type AB red cells by mistake. Which antibodies cause his reaction?","c":["The donor's anti-B antibodies, attacking his own B cells","None; AB is the universal type, so the transfusion is safe","His own anti-B antibodies, attacking the donor's B antigens","His own anti-A antibodies, attacking the A antigens on the donor cells"],"a":3,"why":"The recipient owns the antibodies. A type B man carries anti-A, and AB donor cells wear the A antigen, so his anti-A clumps and bursts them.","no":["AB plasma has no antibodies at all, and in a red cell transfusion the danger is the recipient's antibodies.","AB is the universal RECIPIENT, not a universal donor: AB cells carry both antigens.","He never makes anti-B, because B is his own antigen.",""],"id":"q49"},{"kc":"kc15","q":"A small clinic's blood bank has only type B and type O red cells left. A type A trauma patient needs red cells now. Which unit can she get?","c":["Type O only","Type B only","Either type B or type O","Neither; she can receive only type A"],"a":0,"why":"Her plasma carries anti-B, so type B cells would be attacked. Type O cells carry no A or B antigen, so her antibodies have nothing to bind.","no":["","Her anti-B would clump and burst type B cells.","Type O is safe, but type B is not.","A type A patient can receive type O red cells as well as type A."],"id":"q50"},{"kc":"kc15","q":"A hospital wants plasma that can be given to a patient of any ABO type. Which donor type's plasma fits?","c":["Type O, because O is the universal donor","Type AB, because AB plasma has no anti-A or anti-B","Type A","Type B"],"a":1,"why":"With plasma, the antibodies are the thing in the bag. AB plasma has no anti-A or anti-B, so it cannot attack anyone's red cells: the rule flips, and AB is the universal plasma donor.","no":["O plasma carries both anti-A and anti-B and would attack A, B and AB cells. O is the universal donor of red cells only.","","Type A plasma has anti-B and would attack B and AB cells.","Type B plasma has anti-A and would attack A and AB cells."],"id":"q51"},{"kc":"kc16","q":"Which of these defenses belongs to ADAPTIVE immunity?","c":["Mucus trapping dust in the airways","Complement proteins punching a hole in a bacterium","A neutrophil swallowing bacteria in a splinter wound","A T cell that responds only to one protein of the measles virus"],"a":3,"why":"Adaptive immunity is specific and remembers: each B or T cell recognizes one antigen. A T cell tuned to a single measles protein is exactly that.","no":["Mucus is a physical barrier, part of the innate first line.","Complement is an innate protein cascade; it does not learn or remember.","Neutrophils are innate phagocytes that eat many kinds of bacteria the same way every time.",""],"id":"q52"},{"kc":"kc16","q":"A boy scrapes his knee twice, a month apart, and the same kind of skin bacteria get into both scrapes. Doctors compare how fast neutrophils arrive at the wound and how fast his antibody level rises. What is different the second time?","c":["Both are faster, because the whole immune system learns","Only the antibody response is faster and larger; the neutrophils arrive just as before","Only the neutrophils are faster, because innate immunity is the quick branch","Nothing changes; immune responses are the same every time"],"a":1,"why":"Neutrophils are innate, and innate defenses respond the same way every time. Antibodies come from the adaptive branch, whose memory cells make the second response faster and bigger.","no":["Innate cells such as neutrophils have no memory.","","Innate is fast, but it does not get faster with repeat exposure: that is the classic trap.","The adaptive response improves on re-exposure, thanks to memory."],"id":"q53"},{"kc":"kc16","q":"Natural killer (NK) cells look like lymphocytes and destroy virus-infected cells and cancer cells. Why are they classed as innate, not adaptive?","c":["They make antibodies","They work only on a second exposure","They kill on first contact without recognizing one specific antigen, and they form no memory","They live only in the skin"],"a":2,"why":"NK cells strike infected or cancerous cells on first contact, without needing to have met them before, and they do not improve with repeat exposure. No antigen specificity and no memory means innate.","no":["NK cells do not make antibodies; only plasma cells do.","That is the opposite of what NK cells do; they work right away.","","NK cells travel in the blood and tissues, and location does not decide the branch anyway."],"id":"q54"},{"kc":"kc17","q":"During an infection, a lymph node biopsy shows many cells crammed with rough endoplasmic reticulum and secreting protein nonstop. Which cells are these, and what are they making?","c":["Plasma cells making antibodies","Memory B cells making antibodies","Cytotoxic T cells making antibodies","Macrophages making complement"],"a":0,"why":"Rough ER is protein-export machinery, and the cell built for mass export of antibody is the plasma cell, the effector daughter of an activated B cell.","no":["","Memory B cells wait for the next exposure; they do not actively secrete antibody.","Cytotoxic T cells kill infected cells directly; they never make antibody.","Macrophages are big eaters, not protein-export factories, and most complement comes from the liver."],"id":"q55"},{"kc":"kc17","q":"Multiple myeloma is a cancer in which one plasma cell multiplies out of control. Blood tests show a huge amount of one single antibody. Why only one?","c":["Cancer cells make every class of antibody at once","Memory B cells have stopped working","Helper T cells are secreting the extra antibody","All the cancer cells are one clone, and every cell in a clone secretes the same antibody"],"a":3,"why":"Each B cell, and every plasma cell descended from it, makes antibody of one shape. A cancer grown from a single plasma cell is one clone, so it pours out one identical antibody.","no":["One clone makes one antibody shape, not a mix.","Memory cells do not secrete antibody, so they cannot explain the excess.","Helper T cells send signals; they never secrete antibody.",""],"id":"q56"},{"kc":"kc17","q":"Three months after a bad flu, a man's antibody level against that flu strain has fallen a lot, yet he stays protected against it. What best explains this?","c":["His plasma cells turned into memory cells that keep secreting antibody","Most of his plasma cells have died off, but long-lived memory B cells remain and can quickly make new plasma cells","His cytotoxic T cells took over making the antibody","His neutrophils learned to recognize the flu"],"a":1,"why":"Plasma cells are short-lived effectors, so antibody levels fall once the infection ends. Memory B cells persist without secreting, and on re-exposure they rapidly produce a new wave of plasma cells.","no":["Plasma cells and memory cells are separate daughters of the activated B cell, and memory cells do not secrete.","","Cytotoxic T cells kill infected cells; they never make antibody.","Neutrophils are innate and have no memory."],"id":"q57"},{"kc":"kc18","q":"Mature red blood cells have no nucleus, so they display no class I MHC. The malaria parasite spends part of its life hiding inside red cells. Which defense has the hardest time spotting an infected red cell?","c":["Antibodies binding parasites that burst out into the plasma","Spleen macrophages that remove stiff, damaged red cells","CD8 cytotoxic T cells","CD4 helper T cells reading class II on macrophages that swallowed parasites"],"a":2,"why":"CD8 cytotoxic T cells find infected cells by reading foreign pieces on class I MHC. With no nucleus, a red cell has no class I to read, so the parasite hides from them.","no":["Free parasites in the plasma are outside cells, where antibodies work well.","The spleen screens red cells by their shape and stiffness, not by MHC.","","Macrophages that swallow parasites display the pieces on class II, which CD4 cells can read."],"id":"q58"},{"kc":"kc18","q":"A melanoma cell makes an abnormal protein, and pieces of it appear on the cell's surface. Which pairing lets the immune system kill this cell directly?","c":["Class I MHC read by a CD8 cytotoxic T cell","Class II MHC read by a CD8 cytotoxic T cell","Class I MHC read by a CD4 helper T cell","Class II MHC read by a CD4 helper T cell"],"a":0,"why":"Proteins made inside any nucleated cell are shown on class I MHC, and CD8 cytotoxic T cells read class I and kill. CD8 × I = 8.","no":["","CD8 cells read class I, and a melanoma cell is not an antigen-presenting cell with class II.","CD4 cells read class II, not class I (4 × 1 = 4, not 8).","That pairing is real, but it happens on antigen-presenting cells and leads to helping, not direct killing."],"id":"q59"},{"kc":"kc18","q":"In a lab dish, virus-infected kidney cells are mixed with CD4 helper T cells from the same person. The T cells ignore the infected cells. Why?","c":["Kidney cells carry no MHC of any kind","CD4 cells respond only to bacteria, never to viruses","Viral proteins cannot be displayed on MHC","Kidney cells show viral pieces on class I MHC, but CD4 cells read only class II, found on antigen-presenting cells"],"a":3,"why":"Every nucleated cell, a kidney cell included, shows its inside proteins on class I. CD4 helper T cells read class II, which only antigen-presenting cells (macrophages, dendritic cells, B cells) carry: CD4 × II = 8.","no":["Kidney cells are nucleated, so they carry class I MHC.","CD4 cells help against viruses too, but only after reading class II on an antigen-presenting cell.","Viral pieces are shown on class I all the time; that is how CD8 cells find infected cells.",""],"id":"q60"},{"kc":"kc19","q":"A closed syringe holds 40 mL of air at 760 mmHg. You pull the plunger out to 50 mL. No air gets in, and the temperature stays the same. What is the pressure inside now?","c":["950 mmHg","760 mmHg","608 mmHg","380 mmHg"],"a":2,"why":"Boyle's law: P₁V₁ = P₂V₂, so 760 × 40 = P₂ × 50, and P₂ = 608 mmHg. A bigger volume means a lower pressure.","no":["That flips the ratio (760 × 50 / 40). A bigger volume must give a LOWER pressure, not a higher one.","Pressure stays at 760 only if the volume stays the same or air flows in to fill the extra space.","","Halving the pressure would need the volume to double, from 40 to 80 mL."],"id":"q61"},{"kc":"kc19","q":"Midway through a quiet breath in, a sensor reads an alveolar pressure of 759 mmHg while the room air is at 760 mmHg. What is happening at that moment?","c":["Air is flowing in, from 760 outside toward 759 inside","Air is flowing out, toward the lower pressure","No air moves, because 1 mmHg is too small a difference to matter","Air is flowing in because the lung tissue contracts and pulls it"],"a":0,"why":"Air moves by bulk flow from higher to lower pressure. In quiet breathing a gradient of only about 1 mmHg is enough to bring in a normal breath.","no":["","Air does flow toward lower pressure, and the lower pressure is inside the lung, so the air moves in.","About 1 mmHg is the normal gradient that moves every quiet breath.","Lung tissue has no muscle to contract. The chest wall muscles change the volume, and the pressure gradient does the rest."],"id":"q62"},{"kc":"kc19","q":"A man's diaphragm is paralyzed because both of his phrenic nerves were damaged, but his external intercostals still work. Why is each breath in now much smaller?","c":["His alveoli can no longer pump O₂ into the blood","His chest volume barely increases, so alveolar pressure barely drops below atmospheric","His alveolar pressure rises above atmospheric while he tries to breathe in","His type II cells stop making surfactant"],"a":1,"why":"The diaphragm does most of the volume change in quiet breathing. A small gain in volume gives only a small pressure drop (Boyle's law), so only a small gradient pulls air in.","no":["Alveoli never pump gas; diffusion is passive. His problem is moving air, not exchanging it.","","Alveolar pressure rises above atmospheric during expiration, not while the chest is trying to expand.","Surfactant comes from type II alveolar cells; it does not depend on the phrenic nerve."],"id":"q63"},{"kc":"kc20","q":"Which cell secretes pulmonary surfactant?","c":["Type I alveolar cell","Alveolar macrophage","Goblet cell in the bronchi","Type II alveolar cell"],"a":3,"why":"Type II alveolar cells secrete surfactant, a mix of lipids and proteins that lowers surface tension in the alveoli.","no":["Type I cells form the thin wall that gases diffuse across.","Macrophages patrol the alveoli and clean up debris and microbes.","Goblet cells make airway mucus, not surfactant.",""],"id":"q64"},{"kc":"kc20","q":"Two neighboring alveoli have the same surface tension, but alveolus A has half the radius of alveolus B. There is no surfactant. Using P = 2T/r, what happens?","c":["A has twice the collapsing pressure, so its air tends to empty into B","B has twice the collapsing pressure, so its air empties into A","Both have the same collapsing pressure, so nothing moves","A has half the collapsing pressure, so A stays open more easily"],"a":0,"why":"With T the same, halving r doubles P. The small alveolus squeezes harder, so its air flows into the larger one and it collapses. Surfactant prevents this by lowering T the most in small alveoli.","no":["","B is the larger alveolus, and a larger radius gives a LOWER collapsing pressure.","Equal tension does not mean equal pressure; the radius changes P.","A smaller r gives a larger P, not a smaller one."],"id":"q65"},{"kc":"kc20","q":"A classmate says surfactant \"works like cough syrup: it thins the mucus in the bronchi so air can get through.\" What is the best correction?","c":["That is right: surfactant is a mucolytic in the airways","Surfactant raises surface tension in the airways to hold them open","Surfactant lowers surface tension inside the alveoli; mucus thinners act in the airways","Surfactant is made by goblet cells and dissolves mucus plugs"],"a":2,"why":"Surfactant acts on the water film inside the alveoli, lowering surface tension so they stay open. Breaking down airway mucus is a mucolytic drug's job.","no":["This is the trap: surfactant does not act on mucus at all.","Surfactant LOWERS surface tension, and it works in the alveoli, not the airways.","","Type II alveolar cells make surfactant, and it does not dissolve mucus."],"id":"q66"},{"kc":"kc21","q":"At the end of a quiet breath in, alveolar pressure is back at 760 mmHg. Then the diaphragm and external intercostals relax. A moment later, what is alveolar pressure, and which way does air move?","c":["About 759 mmHg; air moves in","About 760 mmHg; no air moves","About 756 mmHg; air moves out","About 761 mmHg; air moves out"],"a":3,"why":"Elastic recoil makes the lungs smaller, so by Boyle's law alveolar pressure rises to about 761 mmHg, just above atmospheric. Air flows out down that gradient.","no":["759 mmHg is the value during a breath in.","760 mmHg is the value between breaths, when no air moves.","756 mmHg is the usual intrapleural pressure, and an alveolar pressure below 760 would pull air in, not push it out.",""],"id":"q67"},{"kc":"kc21","q":"A patient's abdominal muscles are paralyzed, but her diaphragm and intercostals work. Sitting quietly she breathes normally, yet she cannot cough hard or blow up a balloon. What explains this?","c":["Quiet breathing out never needed the abdominal muscles; only forced expiration does","Her diaphragm can no longer relax","Her lungs have lost their elastic recoil","Her external intercostals now push the air out at rest"],"a":0,"why":"Quiet expiration is passive elastic recoil, so it is unchanged. Forced expiration (a hard cough, blowing hard) needs the abdominal muscles and internal intercostals, and she has lost the abdominal part.","no":["","Paralyzed belly muscles do not stop the diaphragm from relaxing.","Recoil comes from the lungs and chest wall, and it still works. That is why her quiet breathing is normal.","The external intercostals are inspiratory muscles; they relax during expiration."],"id":"q68"},{"kc":"kc21","q":"A runner sprinting uphill breathes out hard and fast. Compared with a quiet breath out at rest, what is different?","c":["At rest the abdominal muscles do the work; while sprinting, elastic recoil takes over","While sprinting, the abdominal muscles and internal intercostals contract using ATP; at rest, elastic recoil works with no contraction","Nothing is different; both are passive recoil, just faster when sprinting","While sprinting, the diaphragm contracts harder to push the air out"],"a":1,"why":"Quiet expiration is passive recoil, paid for by energy stored during inspiration. Forced expiration adds the abdominal muscles (they push the diaphragm up) and the internal intercostals (they pull the ribs down and in), powered by ATP.","no":["This is reversed: at rest no muscle contracts.","","Forced expiration is active: muscles contract and use ATP.","A contracting diaphragm makes the chest bigger, which is inspiration. In forced expiration the abdominal muscles push the relaxed diaphragm up."],"id":"q69"},{"kc":"kc22","q":"Two patients do spirometry. Patient X: total lung capacity 3800 mL (normal about 5700), a small vital capacity, residual volume about normal. Patient Y: residual volume 3000 mL (normal about 1200) and a low FEV₁. Which pairing is correct?","c":["X obstructive, Y restrictive","Both obstructive","X restrictive, Y obstructive","Both restrictive"],"a":2,"why":"A small total lung capacity means the lungs cannot fill: restrictive. A high residual volume and a low FEV₁ mean air is trapped and slow to get out: obstructive.","no":["Reversed: the small TLC is the restrictive sign, and the trapped air is the obstructive sign.","X shows no air trapping; X simply cannot fill.","","Y's high residual volume comes from trapped air, the obstructive sign."],"id":"q70"},{"kc":"kc22","q":"A lung that is hard to stretch has low compliance, so it takes more work to get air in. In which disease is lung compliance LOW?","c":["Asthma","Emphysema","Chronic bronchitis","Pulmonary fibrosis"],"a":3,"why":"Scar tissue makes fibrotic lungs stiff (low compliance), so inflating them takes extra work. That is the restrictive pattern.","no":["Asthma narrows the airways; the lung tissue itself stretches normally.","Emphysema destroys elastic fibers, so compliance goes UP and elastic recoil goes down.","Chronic bronchitis narrows the airways with mucus and inflammation; compliance is about normal.",""],"id":"q71"},{"kc":"kc22","q":"During an asthma attack, a woman can still pull air in, but each breath out is long and wheezy, and her chest still feels full when the next breath starts. Which test result fits best?","c":["A low FEV₁: little air comes out in the first second of a forced breath","A low residual volume","Low lung compliance from scar tissue","A total lung capacity far below normal"],"a":0,"why":"Asthma is obstructive: narrowed bronchioles slow the air on its way out, so FEV₁ falls and air is trapped.","no":["","Air trapping RAISES residual volume.","Scar tissue and low compliance describe fibrosis, the restrictive pattern.","A small total lung capacity is the restrictive sign; in obstructive disease TLC is normal or even larger."],"id":"q72"},{"kc":"kc23","q":"A diver breathes compressed air (21% O₂) at a depth where the total pressure is 1520 mmHg, twice sea level. What is the PO₂ of the air she breathes?","c":["About 160 mmHg","About 1520 mmHg","About 80 mmHg","About 320 mmHg"],"a":3,"why":"Partial pressure = fraction × total pressure: 0.21 × 1520 ≈ 320 mmHg. The percent of O₂ did not change; the total pressure doubled, so PO₂ doubled.","no":["160 mmHg is the PO₂ of air at sea level (0.21 × 760).","1520 mmHg is the total pressure of all the gases together, not the share that belongs to O₂.","That halves the PO₂, but a higher total pressure raises it.",""],"id":"q73"},{"kc":"kc23","q":"Blood reaching a pulmonary capillary has PO₂ 40 mmHg and PCO₂ 46 mmHg. The alveolar air next to it has PO₂ 100 mmHg and PCO₂ 40 mmHg. What are the two gradients?","c":["O₂: 140 mmHg; CO₂: 86 mmHg","O₂: 60 mmHg into the blood; CO₂: 6 mmHg into the alveolus","O₂: 60 mmHg into the alveolus; CO₂: 6 mmHg into the blood","One shared gradient of 54 mmHg for both gases"],"a":1,"why":"100 minus 40 = 60 mmHg pushing O₂ into the blood; 46 minus 40 = 6 mmHg pushing CO₂ into the alveolus. The CO₂ gradient is small, but CO₂ diffuses about 20 times more easily, so enough of it crosses.","no":["A gradient is a difference, not a sum.","","The directions are reversed: each gas moves from its higher to its lower partial pressure.","Each gas has its own gradient. You never subtract one gas from the other."],"id":"q74"},{"kc":"kc23","q":"Blood enters the capillaries of a hard-working thigh muscle with PO₂ 100 mmHg and PCO₂ 40 mmHg. Inside the muscle cells, PO₂ is 20 mmHg and PCO₂ is 55 mmHg. Which way does each gas move?","c":["O₂ into the cells, and CO₂ into the cells","O₂ into the blood, and CO₂ into the cells","O₂ into the cells, and CO₂ into the blood","Neither moves until a carrier protein spends ATP"],"a":2,"why":"Each gas goes from its higher to its lower partial pressure: O₂ from 100 in the blood to 20 in the cells, and CO₂ from 55 in the cells to 40 in the blood. At the tissues the gradients run opposite to the lungs.","no":["CO₂ is higher in the cells (55) than in the blood (40), so it leaves the cells.","O₂ is higher in the blood (100), so it leaves the blood.","","Gas exchange is passive diffusion; no carrier and no ATP are needed."],"id":"q75"},{"kc":"kc23","q":"A man with heart failure has fluid in the thin walls between his alveoli and capillaries (pulmonary edema). His alveolar PO₂ is normal, but his arterial PO₂ is low. Why?","c":["The fluid stops the ATP pumps that load O₂","The thicker barrier slows diffusion, so less O₂ crosses in the time the blood spends in the capillary","The fluid reverses the O₂ gradient, so O₂ leaves the blood","The fluid raises alveolar PCO₂, and the CO₂ blocks O₂ from crossing"],"a":1,"why":"Diffusion is faster with a bigger gradient, more surface area and a thinner wall. Edema thickens the wall, and O₂, which diffuses less easily than CO₂, suffers first.","no":["There are no O₂ pumps; gas exchange is passive diffusion.","","Alveolar PO₂ is still higher than blood PO₂, so O₂ still moves into the blood, just more slowly.","O₂ and CO₂ move independently; each follows its own gradient."],"id":"q76"},{"kc":"kc24","q":"Overnight in the hospital, a patient's arterial PO₂ drifts from 95 mm Hg down to 75 mm Hg. Reading the O₂-hemoglobin curve, what happens to her hemoglobin saturation?","c":["It falls by about 20%, matching the 20 mm Hg drop","It falls by only a few percentage points and stays above 90%","It falls to about 50%, because 75 mm Hg is on the steep part","It rises, because hemoglobin holds O₂ tighter at a lower PO₂"],"a":1,"why":"Both readings sit on the flat top of the curve (60 to 100 mm Hg), where saturation barely changes: about 97% at 95 and about 95% at 75. That flat top is the safety margin for loading.","no":["That is straight-line thinking; the curve is flat here, not proportional.","","The steep part starts below about 60 mm Hg; 75 is still on the plateau.","Saturation does not rise when PO₂ falls; at best it stays level."],"id":"q77"},{"kc":"kc24","q":"At rest, blood enters a tissue capillary about 97.5% saturated and leaves at a PO₂ of 40 mm Hg, about 75% saturated. Roughly what share of its O₂ did the blood hand to the tissue?","c":["About 75%","About half","Almost all of it","About one quarter"],"a":3,"why":"Saturation fell from about 97.5% to 75%, a drop of about 22 points out of 97.5, so the tissue took roughly a quarter. The other three quarters stay on hemoglobin as a reserve that working tissue can draw on.","no":["75% is what is still ON hemoglobin as the blood leaves, not what it gave away.","Hemoglobin falls to half full only at a PO₂ well below 40 mm Hg.","At rest the venous blood is still about three quarters full; most of the O₂ goes back to the heart.",""],"id":"q78"},{"kc":"kc24","q":"PO₂ falls by the same 20 mm Hg in two places: in arterial blood (from 100 to 80 mm Hg) and in the capillaries of a sprinting leg muscle (from 40 to 20 mm Hg). Which statement fits the O₂-hemoglobin curve?","c":["The arterial drop barely changes saturation, but the muscle drop releases a large amount of O₂","Both drops release the same amount of O₂, because the PO₂ change is the same","The arterial drop releases more O₂, because the PO₂ values are higher","Neither drop matters, because hemoglobin releases O₂ only below 20 mm Hg"],"a":0,"why":"From 100 to 80 the curve is flat: saturation goes from about 97.5% to about 96%. From 40 to 20 the curve is steep: saturation falls from about 75% to about a third, so the muscle pulls out a lot of extra O₂ for the same pressure drop.","no":["","Equal PO₂ drops give equal saturation changes only on a straight line; this curve is S-shaped.","This swaps the zones: the flat top is for loading, the steep part for unloading.","Hemoglobin releases O₂ all along the steep part, which starts below about 60 mm Hg."],"id":"q79"},{"kc":"kc25","q":"In a lab, a sample of blood is warmed above body temperature and CO₂ is bubbled through it. At a PO₂ of 40 mm Hg, its saturation now reads about 60% instead of the usual 75%. What happened?","c":["The curve shifted left: hemoglobin now grips O₂ more tightly","The curve did not shift; the sample simply lost hemoglobin","The curve shifted right: affinity fell, so hemoglobin gives up more O₂ at the same PO₂","The curve flattened, so saturation no longer depends on PO₂"],"a":2,"why":"Heat and CO₂ (which forms acid) both lower hemoglobin's affinity for O₂. Lower saturation at the same PO₂ is exactly what a right shift looks like: more O₂ is released.","no":["A left shift would RAISE saturation at a PO₂ of 40, not lower it.","Saturation is a percentage of the hemoglobin present; heat and CO₂ change affinity, not the amount of hemoglobin.","","The curve keeps its S shape; it just moves to the right."],"id":"q80"},{"kc":"kc25","q":"Which change, by itself, shifts the O₂-hemoglobin curve to the RIGHT?","c":["More 2,3-DPG in the red blood cells","A drop in body temperature","A fall in blood PCO₂","A rise in blood pH"],"a":0,"why":"2,3-DPG (Sherwood calls it 2,3-BPG) lowers hemoglobin's affinity, so more of it shifts the curve right. It is the D in CADET.","no":["","Cooling raises affinity and shifts the curve left.","Less CO₂ means less acid, which shifts the curve left.","A higher pH means fewer H⁺ ions, which shifts the curve left."],"id":"q81"},{"kc":"kc25","q":"A fisherman is pulled from an icy lake with a core temperature of 31 °C (normal is 37 °C). How does the cold change the way his hemoglobin handles O₂ in his tissues?","c":["Right shift: hemoglobin releases more O₂ to his cold tissues","No shift: temperature affects enzymes, not hemoglobin","Right shift: hemoglobin holds O₂ more tightly","Left shift: hemoglobin holds O₂ more tightly and unloads less at the tissues"],"a":3,"why":"Cold raises hemoglobin's affinity and shifts the curve left. At any tissue PO₂, hemoglobin stays fuller and gives up less O₂.","no":["Heat shifts the curve right; cold does the opposite.","Temperature is one of the CADET factors; it changes hemoglobin's affinity.","This pairs a right shift with tighter binding, the classic reversal. A right shift means LOOSER binding.",""],"id":"q82"},{"kc":"kc26","q":"A patient takes a drug that blocks carbonic anhydrase. Which way of carrying CO₂ in the blood is slowed the most?","c":["CO₂ dissolved in plasma","Making bicarbonate inside red blood cells","CO₂ carried on the globin chains of hemoglobin","O₂ binding to the heme iron"],"a":1,"why":"Carbonic anhydrase in red blood cells speeds CO₂ + H₂O into H₂CO₃, which splits into H⁺ and HCO₃⁻. Block the enzyme and the main CO₂ route, bicarbonate, slows down.","no":["Dissolving CO₂ in plasma needs no enzyme.","","Carbaminohemoglobin forms when CO₂ binds globin directly; it does not need carbonic anhydrase.","That is O₂ transport, not CO₂ transport, and it needs no carbonic anhydrase."],"id":"q83"},{"kc":"kc26","q":"Red blood cells are sampled from a vein draining a working muscle and from an artery. Which cells contain more chloride (Cl⁻), and why?","c":["The arterial cells, because Cl⁻ follows O₂ into the red cell at the lungs","Neither; red cells never exchange Cl⁻","The venous cells, because Cl⁻ moved in as HCO₃⁻ moved out in the tissue capillaries","The venous cells, because Cl⁻ is made from CO₂ inside the red cell"],"a":2,"why":"In tissue capillaries, HCO₃⁻ made in the red cell leaves for the plasma and Cl⁻ comes in to keep the charges balanced: the chloride shift. At the lungs the swap reverses, so arterial red cells hold less Cl⁻.","no":["Cl⁻ trades places with HCO₃⁻, not with O₂; at the lungs Cl⁻ actually moves OUT of the red cell.","The chloride shift is exactly this exchange across the red cell membrane.","","Cl⁻ is not made from CO₂; it comes in from the plasma."],"id":"q84"},{"kc":"kc26","q":"A study partner says, \"CO₂ on hemoglobin kicks O₂ off by taking its place on the heme iron.\" What is the best correction?","c":["CO₂ binds the globin protein chains (carbaminohemoglobin), not the heme iron where O₂ sits","That is correct: CO₂ and O₂ compete for the same iron site","CO₂ never touches hemoglobin; it travels only as bicarbonate","CO₂ binds the heme iron, but only at the lungs"],"a":0,"why":"Carbaminohemoglobin forms when CO₂ binds the globin chains. O₂ binds the heme iron, so the two are not competing for one seat.","no":["","This is the \"same-site competition\" picture the study guide calls a persistent misconception.","A real share of CO₂ (about 23% in our study guide) rides on hemoglobin as carbaminohemoglobin.","CO₂ does not bind the heme iron anywhere; at the lungs CO₂ is unloaded, not loaded."],"id":"q85"},{"kc":"kc27","q":"A patient's arterial PO₂ is 85 mm Hg. Her arterial PCO₂ then rises from 40 to 46 mm Hg, and her breathing deepens. Which sensors are mainly responsible, and what do they detect?","c":["Peripheral chemoreceptors, detecting her low PO₂","Lung stretch receptors, detecting CO₂ in the alveoli","Central chemoreceptors, detecting CO₂ molecules directly","Central chemoreceptors in the medulla, detecting the H⁺ that CO₂ forms in brain fluid"],"a":3,"why":"The rise in PCO₂ is the main breathing drive: CO₂ enters the brain's extracellular fluid, forms H⁺, and the central chemoreceptors sense that H⁺. A PO₂ of 85 is well above about 60 mm Hg, so the O₂ sensors are barely firing.","no":["A PO₂ of 85 is not low enough; the peripheral O₂ sensors fire hard only below about 60 mm Hg.","Stretch receptors sense how inflated the lung is, not CO₂.","Right place, wrong signal: they respond to H⁺, not to CO₂ itself.",""],"id":"q86"},{"kc":"kc27","q":"A teenager with untreated type 1 diabetes has ketoacids building up in his blood, and he is breathing deep and fast. His arterial PCO₂ is LOW. Which sensors are mainly driving this breathing?","c":["Central chemoreceptors, because blood H⁺ crosses easily into the brain","Peripheral chemoreceptors in the carotid and aortic bodies, which sense the rise in arterial H⁺","Lung stretch receptors, which sense the acid in the airways","No sensors; the acid stimulates the diaphragm directly"],"a":1,"why":"H⁺ from ketoacids does not cross the blood-brain barrier easily, so the central chemoreceptors are not the first to sense it. The carotid and aortic bodies respond to arterial H⁺ and drive breathing up, which blows off CO₂ (that is why his PCO₂ is low).","no":["H⁺ crosses the blood-brain barrier poorly; CO₂ is what crosses easily.","","Stretch receptors sense lung inflation, not acid.","The diaphragm contracts only when the brain stem signals it through the phrenic nerve."],"id":"q87"},{"kc":"kc27","q":"A volunteer in a physiology lab breathes fast and deep on purpose for a minute, and her arterial PCO₂ falls from 40 to 25 mm Hg. What happens next?","c":["Blood pH falls, and the central chemoreceptors fire harder","Her O₂ sensors take over and make her breathe even faster","Blood pH rises, H⁺ in brain fluid falls, and her drive to breathe weakens for a while","Nothing changes, because breathing control watches only O₂"],"a":2,"why":"Blowing off CO₂ leaves less CO₂ to make H⁺, so blood pH rises (respiratory alkalosis). Less H⁺ in brain fluid quiets the central chemoreceptors, so the urge to breathe fades until CO₂ builds back up.","no":["Less CO₂ means less acid, so pH rises, not falls.","Her PO₂ is normal or high, far above the roughly 60 mm Hg where the O₂ sensors kick in.","","CO₂, not O₂, is the main minute-to-minute signal."],"id":"q88"},{"kc":"kc28","q":"Low O₂ is the local signal in two places: around an arteriole in a working calf muscle, and in an alveolus behind a partly blocked airway. How does the nearby arteriole respond in each place?","c":["Calf: dilates. Lung: constricts.","Calf: dilates. Lung: dilates.","Calf: constricts. Lung: dilates.","Calf: constricts. Lung: constricts."],"a":0,"why":"Systemic arterioles dilate to low O₂ to bring blood to tissue that needs it. Pulmonary arterioles do the reverse (hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction), steering blood away from alveoli that have little air.","no":["","This applies the body rule to the lung: the classic trap.","Both responses are backward.","The calf arteriole dilates; in body tissues, low O₂ is a vasodilator signal."],"id":"q89"},{"kc":"kc28","q":"A small tumor presses on a bronchiole and cuts the airflow to one lung region in half, while blood flow to that region is unchanged at first. What happens to the region's V/Q, and what is the local response?","c":["V/Q rises; the local arterioles dilate to bring in more blood","V/Q stays the same, because blood flow did not change","V/Q falls; the local arterioles dilate because the region is short of O₂","V/Q falls; the local arterioles constrict, sending blood to better-aired regions"],"a":3,"why":"Less air (V) with the same blood (Q) means V/Q falls. Alveolar PO₂ drops, the local pulmonary arterioles constrict, and blood moves to regions that have air, bringing V and Q back into balance.","no":["Less ventilation LOWERS V/Q, and low alveolar O₂ constricts lung arterioles.","V dropped, so the ratio changed even though Q did not.","Right ratio, wrong response: dilating is the body rule; in the lung, low O₂ constricts.",""],"id":"q90"},{"kc":"kc28","q":"A patient with one collapsed lung lobe is given an IV drug that relaxes all vascular smooth muscle, including the lung arterioles. His arterial PO₂ falls further. Why?","c":["The drug blocks O₂ from diffusing across the alveolar wall","Blood that had been steered away from the airless lobe now flows through it and returns without picking up O₂","The drug shifts the O₂ curve to the left","Relaxed arterioles raise alveolar ventilation too much"],"a":1,"why":"Hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction normally squeezes the arterioles of the airless lobe. When the drug relaxes them, blood flows past alveoli with no fresh air, comes back low in O₂ and dilutes the well-oxygenated blood, so arterial PO₂ falls.","no":["Diffusion in the working alveoli is unchanged; the problem is where the blood goes.","","Nothing here changes hemoglobin's affinity; the problem is the V/Q mismatch.","Arteriole tone controls blood flow, not airflow; ventilation is unchanged."],"id":"q91"},{"kc":"kc28","q":"A blood clot lodges in the artery to one lung region. The alveoli there still fill with air on every breath but get almost no blood. Which describes this region?","c":["Low V/Q: plenty of blood, too little air","Normal V/Q, because the alveoli still fill with air","High V/Q: air arrives, but almost no blood is there to pick up O₂, so that ventilation is wasted","Low V/Q, so its arterioles constrict to fix it"],"a":2,"why":"V (air) is normal but Q (blood) is near zero, so V/Q is very high. The air that reaches those alveoli does no gas exchange, much like extra dead space.","no":["That describes a blocked airway, not a blocked vessel.","Filling with air is only half of the match; blood must also flow past.","","This region has too little blood, not too little air, and its alveolar O₂ is not low."],"id":"q92"}],"cases":[{"kc":["kc4","kc5","kc1"],"q":"Chart: 68-year-old man, fainted twice this week. Pulse 32 per minute. ECG: P waves march along steadily at 76 per minute, but QRS complexes come at only 32 per minute with no fixed link to the P waves. Echo: stroke volume 80 mL. What broke, and what is his cardiac output?","c":["The SA node failed; CO is about 6,080 mL/min","Signals are not crossing the AV node, so a slow pacemaker below it drives the ventricles; CO is about 2,560 mL/min","The Purkinje fibers are firing too fast; CO is about 2,560 mL/min","The AV node is pacing the ventricles normally; CO is about 6,080 mL/min"],"a":1,"why":"Steady P waves at 76 mean the SA node and atria are fine. QRS complexes at 32 with no link to the P waves mean the signal is blocked at the AV node, and the slow Purkinje fibers (20 to 40 per minute) drive the ventricles. CO = 32 × 80 = 2,560 mL/min, about half of normal, so his brain gets too little blood and he faints.","no":["The steady P waves show the SA node is working. And CO uses the ventricular rate, not the atrial rate.","","At 32 per minute the Purkinje fibers are firing at their normal slow rate, not too fast.","If the AV node passed signals, every P wave would have a QRS and the rate would be 76."],"title":"Fainting with a slow pulse","id":"x1"},{"kc":["kc2"],"q":"Chart: 45-year-old woman, heart transplant 1 year ago. The nerves to her new heart were cut in surgery and have not regrown. On a treadmill, her working leg muscles squeeze more blood back toward her heart. Within the first minute her end-diastolic volume and her stroke volume both rise. What best explains the bigger stroke volume?","c":["Sympathetic nerves to the heart sensed the extra blood and ordered a stronger beat","The vagus nerve released its brake on the heart","The extra filling stretched her cardiac fibers toward optimal length, so each beat was stronger","Her SA node fired faster, and a faster rate always makes each beat bigger"],"a":2,"why":"With her cardiac nerves cut, only an intrinsic mechanism can act this fast. More venous return raises EDV, the fibers are stretched closer to optimal length, and the next beats are stronger: the Frank-Starling law.","no":["Her cardiac nerves are cut. And Frank-Starling needs no nerves anyway.","Her vagus connection is cut too, and changing the rate is not what raised her stroke volume.","","A faster rate leaves less time to fill, which does not make each beat bigger. The rise here came from more filling."],"title":"The new heart on a treadmill","id":"x2"},{"kc":["kc9","kc6"],"q":"Chart: 34-year-old man, motorcycle crash, heavy bleeding from a thigh wound. BP 88/58 mmHg, heart rate 124 beats/min, skin pale and cool, hand veins hard to see. No head injury. What best explains the fast heart and the pale, cool skin?","c":["Pain alone; his blood pressure has nothing to do with it","Low pressure means less stretch, so the baroreceptors fire less; the medulla raises sympathetic output, speeding the heart and constricting skin arterioles","Low pressure makes the baroreceptors fire more, and that extra firing directly speeds the SA node","The parasympathetic system is raising his heart rate and constricting his arterioles"],"a":1,"why":"Blood loss lowers MAP. Less wall stretch means less baroreceptor firing, and the medulla turns sympathetic up and parasympathetic down. Heart rate rises (CO), and skin arterioles constrict (TPR), which makes the skin pale and cool.","no":["Pain can raise heart rate, but the low pressure, pale cool skin and blood loss point to the baroreflex.","","That is the reversal: less pressure means LESS firing, and the receptors do not drive the SA node directly.","Parasympathetic output slows the heart and does not supply most arterioles."],"title":"The pale, racing crash patient","id":"x3"},{"kc":["kc6","kc10","kc9"],"q":"Chart: 22-year-old diver with a spinal cord injury in the neck. BP 78/44 mmHg, heart rate 52 beats/min, skin warm and pink. No bleeding anywhere. The injury has cut the sympathetic pathways that leave the cord below the neck. What broke?","c":["Hemorrhage: his blood volume is too low","His heart muscle is too weak to pump","His baroreceptors stopped working, so the medulla gets no signal","Arterioles and veins lost their sympathetic squeeze: TPR fell and blood pooled in the veins, so MAP fell, and the medulla cannot reach them to fix it"],"a":3,"why":"With sympathetic tone gone, arterioles widen (TPR falls, warm pink skin) and veins relax, so stored blood stays away from the heart (less venous return and CO). MAP ≈ CO × TPR falls. The baroreceptors still sense the drop, but the medulla's sympathetic orders cannot get past the injury, and the intact vagus keeps the heart slow.","no":["There is no bleeding, and his skin is warm and pink, not pale and cool.","Nothing points to damaged heart muscle; the slow rate comes from vagal (parasympathetic) input with no sympathetic opposition.","The baroreceptors in the neck arteries and aortic arch still work; the problem is on the output (efferent) side.",""],"title":"Low pressure after a neck injury","id":"x4"},{"kc":["kc11"],"q":"Chart: 71-year-old woman with right-sided heart failure. Her neck veins bulge, and both ankles swell more by evening; a finger pressed into the skin leaves a dent. Her plasma albumin is normal and her lymph vessels are open. What broke?","c":["Blood backing up raised her venous pressure, so capillary blood pressure rose; more fluid filtered out and less returned, more than the lymph could carry","Low plasma protein weakened the inward pull at the venular end","Histamine made her capillaries leak proteins","Her lymph vessels are blocked, so the normal 3 L/day cannot return"],"a":0,"why":"A failing right heart backs blood up into the veins. Higher venous pressure raises capillary blood pressure, so filtration rises and reabsorption falls. When the surplus is more than the lymph can return, it collects as edema, worst in the ankles because of gravity.","no":["","Her albumin is normal, so the plasma protein pull is normal.","Nothing points to an allergic reaction, burn or injury.","Her lymph vessels are open; the problem is extra filtration."],"title":"Swollen ankles by evening","id":"x5"},{"kc":["kc13"],"q":"A 45-year-old woman flew 14 hours, mostly seated. Two days later her left calf is swollen, warm and painful. On day 4 she has sudden chest pain and shortness of breath, and a scan shows a blocked artery in her lung. What broke?","c":["Her platelets could not stick to collagen, so blood leaked into the calf and lung","A clot formed in a slow-flowing deep leg vein (a thrombus), and a piece broke loose as an embolus that lodged in a lung artery","Her plasmin dissolved a clot too early, so her lung bled","A clot formed in the lung first, then traveled down to the calf"],"a":1,"why":"Sitting still lets blood pool in the leg veins, so a thrombus forms there; a piece that breaks free (an embolus) rides venous blood through the right heart into the lungs. A clot buster such as tPA works by turning on plasmin.","no":["Failed adhesion causes bleeding, not a blocked artery.","","Her problem is a clot where it should not be, not too little clot.","Venous blood flows from the legs toward the heart and lungs, never from the lungs to the calf."],"title":"The long flight","id":"x6"},{"kc":["kc14"],"q":"A 34-year-old woman with very heavy periods has felt exhausted for months and gets winded on short walks. Her fingertip pulse oximeter reads 98% saturation and her arterial PO₂ is normal. Her hemoglobin is 8.5 g/dL (normal about 12 to 16), and her iron stores are low. What broke?","c":["Her lungs fail to load O₂ into the blood","Her kidneys make too much erythropoietin","Iron-deficiency anemia: each hemoglobin is nearly full, but there is too little hemoglobin, so her blood carries too little O₂","Her red cells hold so much hemoglobin that the blood is too thick"],"a":2,"why":"Monthly blood loss drained her iron, so she makes too little hemoglobin. The oximeter and the PO₂ report how full each hemoglobin is and how much O₂ is dissolved, not how much hemoglobin there is: fewer seats, all full.","no":["A normal PO₂ and 98% saturation show her lungs load O₂ well.","Extra EPO would raise her red cell count; her problem is a missing raw material, iron.","","Her hemoglobin is low, not high."],"title":"The 98% reading","id":"x7"},{"kc":["kc17","kc16"],"q":"A 9-month-old boy has had pneumonia twice and three ear infections since he turned 6 months old. His neutrophil and T cell counts are normal. His blood has almost no antibody of any class, and a lymph node biopsy shows no B cells and no plasma cells. What broke?","c":["His B cell line: with no B cells there are no plasma cells, so no antibody is made","His innate defenses: his neutrophils cannot eat bacteria","His cytotoxic T cells, which normally make the antibodies","His class I MHC, so none of his cells can be recognized"],"a":0,"why":"Antibody comes only from plasma cells, the daughters of activated B cells, so no B cells means no antibody. His mother's antibodies, which crossed the placenta, protected him for the first months and then faded.","no":["","His neutrophil count is normal, and innate defenses do not make antibodies anyway.","Cytotoxic T cells kill infected cells and never make antibody; his T cells are normal.","A class I MHC problem would weaken T cell killing of infected cells, not erase every antibody."],"title":"Sick after six months","id":"x8"},{"kc":["kc20","kc19"],"q":"Chart: baby boy, born at 29 weeks. Within an hour he breathes fast and grunts, and the soft wall of his chest caves in with each breath, as if his lungs are very hard to inflate. X-ray: many tiny air sacs collapsed. A lipid and protein mixture is dripped into his breathing tube, and his breathing eases within hours. What broke?","c":["Thick airway mucus; the treatment was a mucus thinner","Too little surfactant from immature type II cells, so high surface tension collapsed his small alveoli","His type I cells could not actively pump O₂ into the blood","His external intercostals were too weak to change his chest volume"],"a":1,"why":"Type II cells make most of their surfactant late in pregnancy. Without it, surface tension stays high, the smallest alveoli collapse, and the stiff lungs need a huge pressure drop for every breath in (newborn respiratory distress syndrome). The treatment replaced the missing surfactant.","no":["Surfactant is not a mucolytic, and his problem is in the alveoli, not the airways.","","No cell pumps O₂; it diffuses passively. And type II cells, not type I, make surfactant.","His muscles are working very hard (the chest wall caves in). The lungs resist because of surface tension."],"title":"The early baby","id":"x9"},{"kc":["kc22","kc23"],"q":"Chart: man, 64, cut stone for 30 years. Short of breath on stairs for a year. His breaths are small and fast. Spirometry: total lung capacity and vital capacity are both well below normal, but the air he does take in comes out quickly. Biopsy: scar tissue in and around the alveolar walls. Arterial PO₂ is low, worst during exercise. What broke?","c":["Narrowed bronchioles trap air, so his problem is getting air out","His abdominal muscles can no longer power quiet expiration","He has lost the ATP pumps that move O₂ across the alveolar wall","Stiff, scarred lungs cannot expand (restrictive), and the thickened walls slow O₂ diffusion into the blood"],"a":3,"why":"Scarring lowers compliance, so the lungs cannot fill: a restrictive pattern with a small TLC and VC but quick emptying. The scar also thickens the barrier between alveolus and capillary, so O₂ diffuses more slowly and arterial PO₂ falls, most of all when exercise rushes blood through the lungs.","no":["That is obstructive disease. His air comes out quickly, and his volumes are small, not trapped.","Quiet expiration uses no muscles; it is elastic recoil.","There are no gas pumps; O₂ crosses by passive diffusion.",""],"title":"The stone cutter","id":"x10"},{"kc":["kc27","kc26"],"q":"ED chart: a 26-year-old woman is found very drowsy after taking extra opioid pain pills. Breathing: 6 shallow breaths per minute. Arterial blood gas: PCO₂ 65 mm Hg (normal 40), pH 7.21 (normal 7.40), PO₂ 62 mm Hg. What broke, and why is her pH low?","c":["Her lungs are making extra CO₂, which lowers the pH","The drug depressed her brain stem breathing drive, so she hypoventilates; CO₂ builds up and forms H⁺, and the pH falls (respiratory acidosis)","She is hyperventilating, which blows off CO₂ and lowers the pH","Her low O₂ is the cause of the low pH, because O₂ is an acid"],"a":1,"why":"Opioids blunt the medulla's breathing centers and their response to CO₂. Breathing too little lets CO₂ pile up; carbonic anhydrase turns it into H⁺ and HCO₃⁻, so the pH drops: respiratory acidosis.","no":["Cells make CO₂; the lungs only remove it, and here they are removing too little.","","6 shallow breaths a minute is HYPOventilation, and blowing off CO₂ would RAISE the pH.","O₂ is not an acid; the extra H⁺ comes from the retained CO₂."],"title":"The slow breather","id":"x11"},{"kc":["kc28"],"q":"Pediatric chart: a 3-year-old choked on a peanut an hour ago. X-ray: the peanut blocks the bronchus to her right lower lobe, so that lobe gets no fresh air. A scan shows blood flow to the blocked lobe has dropped sharply while flow to the other lobes has risen. Her saturation is 93%, only a little below normal. What explains the shift in blood flow?","c":["Low O₂ in the blocked lobe dilated its arterioles, as it does in other tissues","The heart is pumping less blood to the right lung on purpose","The peanut is pressing directly on the lobe's arteries","Low alveolar PO₂ in the blocked lobe constricted its pulmonary arterioles, steering blood to aired lobes and limiting the fall in saturation"],"a":3,"why":"Hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction: the airless lobe's low alveolar PO₂ squeezes its own arterioles, so most of the blood goes where there is air. That V/Q matching is why her saturation drops only a little.","no":["That is the body rule; in the lung low O₂ constricts, and the scan shows LESS flow to the lobe, not more.","The heart pumps one output to both lungs; the local arterioles redistribute it.","The peanut sits in an airway, not a blood vessel.",""],"title":"The peanut","id":"x12"}],"order":[{"kc":["kc3"],"title":"One heartbeat, filling to filling","prompt":"Start with the ventricle filling. Put one cardiac cycle in order.","steps":["Filling: AV valves open, semilunar valves closed, volume rises to EDV","The ventricles start to contract; their pressure beats atrial pressure and the AV valves close (S1)","Isovolumetric contraction: all four valves closed, volume constant, pressure rising steeply","Ventricular pressure beats aortic pressure and the semilunar valves open","Ejection: blood leaves and volume falls to ESV","The ventricles relax; their pressure drops below aortic pressure and the semilunar valves close (S2)","Isovolumetric relaxation: all four valves closed, volume constant, pressure falling","Ventricular pressure drops below atrial pressure, the AV valves open, and filling begins again"],"why":"Every valve moves because of a pressure difference. S1 starts systole by closing the AV valves; S2 ends it by closing the semilunar valves.","id":"o1"},{"kc":["kc4","kc5"],"title":"The path of one beat's signal","prompt":"Put the electrical path of one heartbeat in order, with the ECG part each step makes.","steps":["The SA node drifts to threshold and fires","The signal spreads across both atria (P wave)","The atria contract while the signal waits at the AV node for about 0.1 second (PR segment)","The signal runs down the bundle of His and the bundle branches","Purkinje fibers spread it through the ventricles (QRS complex)","The ventricles contract","The ventricles repolarize (T wave) and then relax"],"why":"Each electrical event comes just before the mechanical event it triggers. The AV pause lets the atria finish filling the ventricles before the ventricles fire.","id":"o2"},{"kc":["kc9","kc6"],"title":"The baroreflex when pressure falls","prompt":"Blood pressure suddenly drops. Put the baroreflex in order, from the drop to the fix.","steps":["Mean arterial pressure falls","The walls of the carotid sinus and aortic arch are stretched less","Baroreceptors fire fewer action potentials","The cardiovascular control center in the medulla reads \"pressure is low\"","Sympathetic output rises and parasympathetic output falls","Heart rate and contractility rise; veins and arterioles constrict","Cardiac output and TPR rise","MAP returns toward normal"],"why":"Detect, signal, process, act, result. The key step is the third one: less stretch means LESS firing, and the medulla reads that drop as low pressure.","id":"o3"},{"kc":["kc11"],"title":"The path of the leftover fluid","prompt":"Follow fluid that leaves a capillary and is not reabsorbed. Put its journey in order.","steps":["At the arteriolar end, capillary blood pressure beats the plasma protein pull","Fluid is filtered out into the interstitial space","At the venular end, the protein pull wins and most of the fluid is reabsorbed","The leftover (about 3 L/day for the whole body) enters blind-ended lymphatic capillaries","Lymph flows through lymph vessels and lymph nodes","Lymph empties into the venous blood near the heart"],"why":"About 20 L/day filters out and about 17 L/day comes back. The lymph returns the rest. Block any later step and the fluid stays behind as edema.","id":"o4"},{"kc":["kc12","kc13"],"title":"From a cut to cleanup","prompt":"A finger is cut. Put hemostasis in order, from the cut to the clot being removed.","steps":["The cut vessel constricts at once (vascular spasm)","Von Willebrand factor bridges platelets to the exposed collagen (adhesion)","Stuck platelets change shape and release ADP and thromboxane A₂ (activation)","More platelets pile on and release more signals (aggregation, positive feedback)","The coagulation cascade turns prothrombin into thrombin","Thrombin turns fibrinogen into a fibrin mesh that locks the plug in place","After the wall heals, plasmin digests the fibrin and the clot dissolves"],"why":"Squeeze, plug, glue, then clean up. The platelet plug comes before and apart from the cascade; thrombin builds the fibrin, and plasmin removes it.","id":"o5"},{"kc":["kc16","kc17"],"title":"From a vaccine to protection","prompt":"A person gets a vaccine against a virus. Put the B cell response in order, from the shot to protection years later.","steps":["The vaccine brings a harmless piece of the virus into the body","The one B cell whose receptor fits that piece binds it (clonal selection)","A CD4 helper T cell gives that B cell the go-ahead","The B cell divides into a clone","Most daughters become plasma cells and secrete antibody","A few daughters become long-lived memory B cells","Over the following months most plasma cells die and antibody levels fall","When the real virus arrives, memory cells launch a fast, large response"],"why":"Selection, help, cloning, then two kinds of daughters: plasma cells that make antibody now, and memory cells that make the next response faster. Memory is what makes this adaptive.","id":"o6"},{"kc":["kc19","kc21"],"title":"One quiet breath, in and out","prompt":"Put one quiet breath in order, from the start of the breath in to the end of the breath out.","steps":["Diaphragm and external intercostals contract","Chest and lungs get bigger","Alveolar pressure falls to about 759 mmHg (Boyle's law)","Air flows in until alveolar pressure is back to 760 mmHg","The inspiratory muscles relax","Elastic recoil makes the lungs smaller","Alveolar pressure rises to about 761 mmHg","Air flows out until alveolar pressure is back to 760 mmHg"],"why":"Every step is volume, then pressure, then flow. Breathing in costs muscle work; breathing out at rest is free, paid for by the elastic energy stored on the way in.","id":"o7"},{"kc":["kc23"],"title":"The trip of one O₂ molecule","prompt":"Follow one O₂ molecule from the room air to a working muscle cell and back to the lungs in the blood.","steps":["Inhaled in room air (PO₂ about 160 mmHg)","Mixed into alveolar air (PO₂ about 100 mmHg)","Diffuses into pulmonary capillary blood that arrived at PO₂ about 40 mmHg","Blood leaves the lungs matched to the alveoli (PO₂ about 100 mmHg)","Arterial blood reaches a muscle capillary","O₂ diffuses out into the muscle cells (PO₂ below 40 mmHg)","Blood heads back to the lungs in the veins at PO₂ about 40 mmHg"],"why":"At every step O₂ moves downhill, from higher to lower PO₂, by passive diffusion. Working cells keep their own PO₂ low by using O₂, so the gradient never runs out.","id":"o8"},{"kc":["kc26"],"title":"CO₂ from cell to exhaled breath","prompt":"Follow one CO₂ molecule from a working cell to the air you breathe out, by the bicarbonate route. Put the steps in order.","steps":["A working cell makes CO₂, which diffuses into the capillary blood","CO₂ enters a red blood cell","Carbonic anhydrase joins CO₂ and H₂O into H₂CO₃","H₂CO₃ splits into H⁺ (buffered by hemoglobin) and HCO₃⁻","HCO₃⁻ leaves for the plasma as Cl⁻ moves in (the chloride shift)","At the lungs, HCO₃⁻ moves back into the red cell and Cl⁻ moves out","The reaction runs backward and CO₂ re-forms","CO₂ diffuses into the alveolus (46 to 40 mm Hg) and is breathed out"],"why":"Carbonic anhydrase in the red cell makes bicarbonate, the chloride shift moves it into the plasma for the trip, and at the lungs every step reverses so CO₂ can leave down its own gradient.","id":"o9"}],"tables":[{"kc":["kc1"],"title":"Working the cardiac output equations","head":["Person","EDV","ESV","SV = EDV − ESV","HR","CO = HR × SV"],"rows":[["Typical resting adult","120 mL","50 mL","70 mL","72 per min","5,040 mL/min (about 5 L)"],["Trained athlete at rest","160 mL","55 mL","105 mL","48 per min","5,040 mL/min (same CO, slower rate)"],["Hard exercise","150 mL","30 mL","120 mL","160 per min","19,200 mL/min (about 19 L)"],["Weakened heart (heart failure)","180 mL","135 mL","45 mL","95 per min","4,275 mL/min (low despite a big EDV)"]],"id":"t1"},{"kc":["kc3"],"title":"Valves, volume and pressure in the four phases","head":["Phase","AV valves","Semilunar valves","Volume","Ventricular pressure"],"rows":[["Filling","Open","Closed","Rising to EDV","Low"],["Isovolumetric contraction","Closed (S1 at the start)","Closed","Constant","Rising steeply"],["Ejection","Closed","Open","Falling to ESV","Above aortic pressure"],["Isovolumetric relaxation","Closed","Closed (S2 at the start)","Constant","Falling"]],"id":"t2"},{"kc":["kc8","kc10"],"title":"Four vessels, four jobs","head":["Vessel","Main job","What makes it fit","Share of blood at rest"],"rows":[["Arteries","Pressure reservoir: keep blood moving between beats","Thick elastic walls stretch in systole and recoil in diastole","About 13%"],["Arterioles","Resistance vessels: set TPR and divide up cardiac output","Thick smooth muscle changes a small radius (R ∝ 1/r⁴)","About 2%"],["Capillaries","Exchange with the tissues","One cell thick, huge total surface area, slow flow, no smooth muscle","About 5%"],["Veins","Blood reservoir (capacitance vessels)","Thin, stretchy walls, large radius, one-way valves","About 64%"]],"id":"t3"},{"kc":["kc11"],"title":"Four ways to get edema","head":["Cause","What changes at the capillary","Example"],"rows":[["Too little plasma protein","Weaker inward pull, so less fluid is reabsorbed","Protein lost in the urine (kidney disease), liver failure, starvation"],["Leaky capillaries","Proteins escape into the tissue, so the inward pull weakens and more fluid leaves","Histamine in an allergic reaction, a burn"],["High venous pressure","Capillary blood pressure rises, so more fluid filters out and less returns","Heart failure, standing still for hours"],["Blocked lymph vessels","The daily surplus (about 3 L) cannot return to the blood","Tumor, lymph nodes removed in surgery, parasites (elephantiasis)"]],"id":"t4"},{"kc":["kc12","kc13"],"title":"Hemostasis: the players and what acts on them","head":["Player","Job","Drug or disorder that acts on it"],"rows":[["Von Willebrand factor","Bridges platelets to exposed collagen (adhesion)","Von Willebrand disease: too little vWF"],["ADP and thromboxane A₂","Activate platelets and recruit more (aggregation)","Aspirin blocks thromboxane A₂"],["Clotting factors from the liver","Run the cascade to thrombin; many need vitamin K","Warfarin, or vitamin K deficiency"],["Factor VIII","One link in the cascade","Hemophilia A: factor VIII missing"],["Thrombin","Turns fibrinogen into fibrin (builds the clot)","Heparin, which helps antithrombin shut it off"],["Plasmin","Digests fibrin (removes the clot)","tPA, the clot buster, turns it on"]],"id":"t5"},{"kc":["kc15"],"title":"ABO blood types","head":["Type","Antigens on red cells","Antibodies in plasma","Can receive red cells from","Can give red cells to"],"rows":[["O","None","Anti-A and anti-B","O only","Everyone (universal donor)"],["A","A","Anti-B","A, O","A, AB"],["B","B","Anti-A","B, O","B, AB"],["AB","A and B","None","Everyone (universal recipient)","AB only"]],"id":"t6"},{"kc":["kc18"],"title":"The two MHC classes","head":["","Class I MHC","Class II MHC"],"rows":[["Found on","Every nucleated cell","Antigen-presenting cells only (macrophages, dendritic cells, B cells)"],["Shows pieces of","Proteins made inside the cell, such as a virus's","What the cell swallowed"],["Read by","CD8 cytotoxic T cells","CD4 helper T cells"],["What happens next","The T cell kills the presenting cell (perforin, granzymes)","The T cell activates B cells and cytotoxic T cells"],["Check","CD8 × I = 8","CD4 × II = 8"]],"id":"t7"},{"kc":["kc19","kc21"],"title":"Breathing in, breathing out, breathing out hard","head":["","Quiet inspiration","Quiet expiration","Forced expiration"],"rows":[["Muscles","Diaphragm + external intercostals contract","None: the inspiratory muscles relax","Abdominal muscles + internal intercostals contract"],["Energy source","ATP for muscle contraction","Elastic recoil stored during inspiration","ATP for muscle contraction"],["Chest volume","Increases","Decreases","Decreases more, and faster"],["Alveolar pressure","Falls to about 759 mmHg","Rises to about 761 mmHg","Rises far above atmospheric"],["Air flow","In","Out","Out, fast and large"]],"id":"t8"},{"kc":["kc22"],"title":"Restrictive vs obstructive at a glance","head":["Feature","Restrictive (pulmonary fibrosis)","Obstructive (asthma, COPD)"],"rows":[["Main problem","Getting air IN","Getting air OUT"],["Lung tissue","Stiff and scarred: low compliance","Normal, or high compliance (emphysema)"],["Airways","Normal","Narrowed (spasm, inflammation, mucus, lost support)"],["Total lung capacity","Reduced","Normal or increased"],["Residual volume","About normal","Increased (air trapping)"],["Vital capacity","Reduced","Reduced"],["FEV₁","Reduced along with the small vital capacity","Reduced: air comes out slowly"],["Memory hook","Cannot fill","Cannot empty"]],"id":"t9"},{"kc":["kc25","kc24"],"title":"Right shift or left shift?","head":["","Right shift","Left shift"],"rows":[["Hemoglobin's affinity for O₂","Lower (looser grip)","Higher (tighter grip)"],["O₂ released at the tissues","More","Less"],["Saturation at a tissue PO₂ of 40 mm Hg","Below the usual 75%","Above the usual 75%"],["Caused by or seen with","More CO₂, more acid (lower pH), more 2,3-DPG, exercise, fever or heat","Less CO₂, less acid (higher pH), less 2,3-DPG, cold, fetal hemoglobin"],["Memory hook","CADET, face Right: Right = Release","Left = Loads"]],"id":"t10"},{"kc":["kc26"],"title":"Three ways CO₂ travels","head":["Form","Share of blood CO₂","How it works"],"rows":[["Bicarbonate (HCO₃⁻)","About 70% (Sherwood: about 60%)","Carbonic anhydrase in the red cell makes H₂CO₃, which splits into H⁺ and HCO₃⁻; HCO₃⁻ leaves for the plasma (chloride shift)"],["Carbaminohemoglobin","About 23% (Sherwood: about 30%)","CO₂ binds the globin protein chains, NOT the heme iron"],["Dissolved CO₂","About 7 to 10%","Dissolved in plasma as a gas"]],"id":"t11"},{"kc":["kc28"],"title":"Low O₂: body vessels vs lung vessels","head":["","Body (systemic) arterioles","Lung (pulmonary) arterioles"],"rows":[["Response to low O₂","Dilate","Constrict (hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction)"],["Where the low O₂ is","In the tissue around the vessel (for example, a working muscle)","In the alveolus the vessel serves (for example, behind a blocked airway)"],["Purpose","Bring more blood to tissue that needs O₂","Send blood away from alveoli with little air, toward alveoli with plenty"],["Result","Blood flow matches the tissue's demand","Perfusion matches ventilation (V/Q matching)"]],"id":"t12"}],"explain":[{"kc":["kc2"],"prompt":"A friend says, \"When more blood comes back to the heart, the nerves notice and tell the heart to squeeze harder.\" Correct them like a physiologist.","model":"The automatic response to extra returning blood is the Frank-Starling law, and it needs no nerves or hormones: it is intrinsic. More venous return fills the ventricle more and raises EDV. The extra stretch moves the cardiac fibers closer to their optimal length, so the next contraction is stronger and SV rises. Sympathetic nerves can also strengthen the beat, but that is extrinsic control: they add Ca²⁺ so the ventricle empties more, lowering ESV.","points":["Names the Frank-Starling law as intrinsic (no nerves or hormones)","More venous return raises EDV","Stretch moves the fibers toward optimal length","Stronger contraction and a bigger SV","Contrasts sympathetic control: more Ca²⁺, lower ESV"],"id":"e1"},{"kc":["kc5","kc3"],"prompt":"Line up the ECG with the pump. Explain what the ventricles do after the QRS complex and after the T wave, and where S1 and S2 fall.","model":"The QRS complex is ventricular depolarization, so the ventricles begin to contract right after it. Their pressure rises above atrial pressure and the AV valves close, which is S1; after isovolumetric contraction the semilunar valves open and ejection happens. The T wave is ventricular repolarization, so the ventricles then relax. Their pressure falls below aortic pressure and the semilunar valves close, which is S2. Each electrical event comes just before the mechanical event it triggers.","points":["QRS = ventricular depolarization, then contraction","S1 = AV valves close as contraction starts","T wave = ventricular repolarization, then relaxation","S2 = semilunar valves close at the end of ejection","Electrical comes just before mechanical"],"id":"e2"},{"kc":["kc8","kc7"],"prompt":"A classmate says, \"If an arteriole opens up a little, blood flow only goes up a little.\" Correct them, using numbers.","model":"Flow = ΔP ÷ R, and resistance goes with 1/r⁴, so any change in radius is multiplied four times over. If an arteriole's radius increases by 50 percent (×1.5), 1.5⁴ ≈ 5, so resistance falls to about 1/5 and flow rises about 5 times at the same gradient. If the radius halves, resistance rises 16 times and flow falls to 1/16. That is why the arterioles, whose smooth muscle adjusts their radius, are the main resistance vessels and control the flow to each organ.","points":["Flow = ΔP ÷ R","Resistance ∝ 1/r⁴ (not a straight line)","A worked number, such as 1.5 times the radius giving about 5 times the flow, or half the radius giving 1/16","Arterioles change their radius with smooth muscle, so they are the main resistance vessels"],"id":"e3"},{"kc":["kc10","kc6","kc9"],"prompt":"During the baroreflex, the sympathetic system squeezes both the veins and the arterioles. Explain how each squeeze raises MAP, and why they work through different terms of MAP ≈ CO × TPR.","model":"Veins are capacitance vessels that hold about 60 to 70 percent of the blood. Squeezing them shifts that stored blood toward the heart, so venous return and EDV rise, and by the Frank-Starling mechanism stroke volume and cardiac output rise. Arterioles are the resistance vessels, so squeezing them narrows their radius and raises TPR. Both raise MAP ≈ CO × TPR: venoconstriction through CO, arteriolar constriction through TPR.","points":["Veins store most of the blood (capacitance vessels)","Venoconstriction raises venous return and EDV","Frank-Starling: more filling, bigger SV, more CO","Arteriolar constriction raises TPR","Venoconstriction does not meaningfully raise TPR"],"id":"e4"},{"kc":["kc12","kc13"],"prompt":"A friend says aspirin and warfarin are \"the same kind of blood thinner.\" Explain where each one acts in hemostasis, and why neither one dissolves a clot that is already there.","model":"Aspirin acts on the platelet plug: it stops platelets from making thromboxane A₂, so activated platelets recruit fewer new platelets and aggregation is weaker. Warfarin acts on the coagulation cascade: it blocks the vitamin K the liver needs to make prothrombin and other clotting factors, so less thrombin forms and less fibrin is laid down. Both only slow the building of new clots. Removing a clot that already exists is plasmin's job, which is why a clot buster such as tPA works by turning plasmin on.","points":["Aspirin blocks thromboxane A₂ in platelets","So aspirin weakens aggregation (the platelet plug)","Warfarin blocks the vitamin K-dependent clotting factors made in the liver","Less thrombin means less fibrin (the cascade)","Only plasmin dissolves an existing clot (tPA turns it on)"],"id":"e5"},{"kc":["kc14"],"prompt":"Extra oxygen by mask helps a patient whose pneumonia keeps O₂ from getting into the blood far more than it helps a patient with severe anemia and healthy lungs. Explain why.","model":"Almost all blood O₂ rides on hemoglobin; only a small amount dissolves in plasma, and PO₂ measures that dissolved part. In pneumonia the PO₂ is low, so hemoglobin leaves the lungs only partly loaded; raising the inhaled O₂ raises the PO₂ and fills those empty seats. In anemia the PO₂ is already normal and the few hemoglobins are already nearly full, so extra O₂ adds only a little dissolved O₂. The anemic patient needs more hemoglobin (more seats), not more pressure.","points":["Nearly all O₂ is carried on hemoglobin","PO₂ reflects dissolved O₂ only","Pneumonia: low PO₂, hemoglobin not fully loaded, so extra O₂ helps","Anemia: normal PO₂ and hemoglobin already full, so extra O₂ adds little","Anemia is a capacity problem, not a pressure problem"],"id":"e6"},{"kc":["kc17","kc18"],"prompt":"A classmate says, \"Cytotoxic T cells make the antibodies that kill viruses.\" Correct the statement like a physiologist.","model":"Cytotoxic (CD8) T cells make no antibodies. They read class I MHC, which every nucleated cell carries, and when a cell shows a foreign piece such as a viral protein they kill that whole cell with perforin and granzymes. Antibodies come from plasma cells, the short-lived daughters of activated B cells, packed with rough ER. Antibodies work outside cells, tagging and clumping germs, so a virus hiding inside a cell needs the CD8 killers.","points":["CD8 cytotoxic T cells do not make antibodies","They read class I MHC and kill the infected cell","They kill with perforin and granzymes","Antibodies are secreted by plasma cells, from the B cell line","Antibodies work outside cells; CD8 cells reach viruses inside cells"],"id":"e7"},{"kc":["kc19","kc21"],"prompt":"Your friend says, \"When I breathe in, my lungs suck the air in, and when I breathe out at rest, my belly muscles squeeze it out.\" Fix both halves.","model":"Neither half is right. To breathe in, the diaphragm and external intercostals contract and make the chest bigger, and the lungs are pulled out with it; by Boyle's law alveolar pressure falls about 1 mmHg below atmospheric, so air flows in down the gradient. The lungs have no muscle and suck nothing. To breathe out at rest, the inspiratory muscles simply relax, the stretched lungs and chest wall recoil, alveolar pressure rises to about 761 mmHg, and air flows out with no muscle contraction. The belly muscles and internal intercostals join in only for forced expiration, such as coughing or hard exercise.","points":["Breath in: diaphragm + external intercostals make the chest bigger","Boyle's law: bigger volume, lower alveolar pressure (about 759 vs 760 mmHg)","Air moves down the pressure gradient; nothing sucks it in","Quiet breath out: passive elastic recoil raises alveolar pressure (about 761 mmHg)","Abdominal muscles + internal intercostals only for forced expiration"],"id":"e8"},{"kc":["kc23"],"prompt":"A friend says the lungs must \"pump\" oxygen into the blood and push carbon dioxide out, because the body needs so much oxygen. Explain, with partial pressures, why no pump is needed.","model":"Gas exchange is passive diffusion, so no pump and no ATP are used. Alveolar air has a PO₂ of about 100 to 104 mmHg and the blood arriving from the body has a PO₂ of about 40, so O₂ diffuses into the blood. That same blood has a PCO₂ of about 46 while the alveoli sit at about 40, so CO₂ diffuses out into the alveoli. Each gas follows its own gradient, on its own, and the tissues keep the gradients alive by using O₂ and making CO₂.","points":["Passive diffusion: no pump, no ATP","O₂: alveoli about 100 to 104 vs arriving blood 40, so into the blood","CO₂: arriving blood 46 vs alveoli 40, so into the alveoli","Each gas moves independently down its own gradient","Cells using O₂ and making CO₂ keep the gradients going"],"id":"e9"},{"kc":["kc24","kc25"],"prompt":"A rower starts an all-out sprint. Explain how the shape of the O₂-hemoglobin curve and a right shift work together to get extra O₂ to her working muscles, while her blood still loads fully in the lungs.","model":"In the lungs, PO₂ is about 100 mm Hg, on the flat top of the curve, so hemoglobin loads to about 97.5% even if PO₂ dips a little. In her working muscles, PO₂ falls toward 20 mm Hg, on the steep part, so a small extra drop makes hemoglobin release a large amount of O₂ (saturation falls from about 75% at rest toward about a third). The muscle also makes heat, CO₂ and acid, which shift the curve right: affinity falls, so even more O₂ is released at the same PO₂. Back in the lungs, CO₂ leaves, the shift reverses, and loading stays nearly full.","points":["Lungs sit on the flat top (60 to 100 mm Hg), so loading is protected","Working muscle sits on the steep part: a small PO₂ drop releases a lot of O₂","Heat, CO₂ and acid shift the curve right","Right shift = lower affinity = more O₂ released at the same PO₂"],"id":"e10"},{"kc":["kc27","kc24"],"prompt":"Explain why your breathing is set minute to minute by CO₂ rather than by O₂, and what the central chemoreceptors actually detect.","model":"Normal arterial PO₂ sits on the flat top of the O₂ curve, so small changes in breathing barely change how much O₂ the blood carries, and the peripheral O₂ sensors (carotid and aortic bodies) fire strongly only below about 60 mm Hg. CO₂, by contrast, changes quickly with every change in breathing and directly changes the acid level. CO₂ crosses the blood-brain barrier easily (H⁺ does not), reacts with water in the brain's extracellular fluid to form H⁺, and the central chemoreceptors in the medulla sense that H⁺ and adjust breathing. CO₂ is the stimulus; H⁺ is the signal.","points":["CO₂, not O₂, is the main minute-to-minute driver","O₂ sensors (carotid and aortic bodies) are a backup, strong only below about 60 mm Hg","CO₂ crosses the blood-brain barrier; H⁺ does not","CO₂ + H₂O forms H⁺ in the brain's extracellular fluid","Central chemoreceptors in the medulla detect that H⁺ and raise ventilation"],"id":"e11"},{"kc":["kc28"],"prompt":"A classmate says, \"Low O₂ always opens blood vessels.\" Explain where that rule holds, where it flips, and why the flip makes sense.","model":"In body (systemic) tissues the rule holds: low O₂ around a working muscle dilates its arterioles, so more blood arrives where O₂ is needed. In the lung it flips: low alveolar PO₂ makes the local pulmonary arterioles constrict (hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction). Blood sent past an alveolus with no fresh air would go back to the heart without picking up O₂, so the lung steers blood toward well-ventilated alveoli instead. That matches perfusion to ventilation (V/Q matching) and keeps arterial PO₂ up.","points":["Systemic arterioles dilate to low O₂","Pulmonary arterioles constrict to low alveolar O₂ (hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction)","Blood through an unaired alveolus would leave without O₂","Blood is redirected to well-ventilated alveoli","Purpose: match perfusion (Q) to ventilation (V)"],"id":"e12"}],"decks":[]};
